TP.register({id:"lp5-g8",lp:5,grade:"8",mode:"curriculum",checks:4,nextGrade:"high school",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="Depot Four";const STORY=`<p>A depot is a stack of food and fuel left out on the ice so a team can pick it up on the way home. On this route each depot sat at the end of a line of black flags, one flag every hundred meters, because a white flag on white snow is no flag at all.</p>
<p>Mira had counted flags all morning. Twenty-six of them, and then the orange crate of Depot Four, half buried and tipped on its side.</p>
<p>“Tell me the number,” Dr. Oyelaran said. He was on his knees in the snow with his glove off, which he never did.</p>
<p>She lifted the fuel can. It was light. A seam had split somewhere in the cold, and the kerosene had drained into the snow and frozen there in a brown stain the shape of a hand. “One day,” she said. “Maybe a day and a half.”</p>
<p>The station rule was taped beside the radio in every tent: Any team below two days of fuel turns back. No exceptions. Fuel was not for warmth alone. Fuel melted snow, and a team that cannot melt snow cannot drink.</p>
<p>Twelve kilometers south, flagged and waiting, was the drill site. Three seasons of grant money had gone into getting a team to that spot. Dr. Oyelaran had written the proposal himself, twice, after the first one was turned down.</p>
<p>“Twelve kilometers,” he said. “Six hours out, six back. We would be at the site by dark.”</p>
<p>“And then what do we drink?”</p>
<p>He did not answer. The wind had begun to move the loose snow in long ribbons across the surface, which is the way it starts.</p>
<p>Mira keyed the radio. Her thumb stayed on the button a moment without pressing. Out on the open ice behind them, a line of black flags ran back toward the station like stitches in cloth.</p>
<p>“Depot Four, this is sledge team two,” she said. “Fuel is down. We are turning for home.”</p>
<p>Dr. Oyelaran stood, pulled his glove on, and took the front traces of the sled without a word. Two kilometers along he said, “The site will still be there in November.”</p>`;const story=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${STORY_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${STORY}</div>`;const ICE_TITLE="How Thick Is the Ice?";const ICE=`<p>A ship can push through sea ice up to a point. Past that point the ice wins, so every polar expedition spends part of its season measuring thickness. The old method is a drill and a tape. A field team walks a line, bores a hole every fifty meters, drops a hooked tape through the hole, and pulls up until the hook catches the bottom of the ice. The number on the tape is the thickness. One line of forty holes takes a team most of a day in air that can sit at thirty degrees below zero.</p>
<p>The newer method never breaks the surface. An instrument towed behind a sled sends a pulse of current into the ice and reads the signal that comes back. Salt water carries current well and ice does not, so the instrument can tell where the ice stops and the ocean begins. A sled crew can read a thirty kilometer line in an afternoon.</p>
<p>Speed comes with a cost. The towed instrument reports an average over a patch of ice several meters wide. It smooths over a narrow crack or a pressure ridge, which is a wall of broken ice pushed up where two floes have collided. A drill hole measures one exact spot and tells you nothing about the ice between holes.</p>
<p>Most teams now use both. A sled crew runs the instrument along a route, and then the field team drills a short line of check holes wherever the instrument readings change fast. Where the two methods disagree by more than ten centimeters, the drill number is the one that goes in the record book.</p>`;const iceText=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${ICE_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${ICE}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows,cap)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:14px auto;font-size:1.3rem">${cap?`<caption style="font-size:1.1rem;padding-bottom:6px">${cap}</caption>`:""}<tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td style="text-align:center">${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;const srcCard=(tag,head,body)=>`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${tag}</h3><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`;const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.55rem">Research question: How does a polar field camp get drinking water?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard("Source A","“Water from Snow,” an article by Dr. Hana Lindqvist, polar logistics engineer. Polar Field Quarterly, March 2026.","A field camp has no liquid water, so every drop is melted on a stove. Melting and warming enough snow for one person for one day burns about 0.4 liters of kerosene. Crews cut blocks from clean drifted snow upwind of the tents and never from the trodden snow near the camp line.")}${srcCard("Source B","A post on a travel forum by a user named IceTrekker_99. No date.","I read somewhere that polar camps just boil sea water and drink it. Pretty sure the salt boils off. Seems way easier than messing with snow.")}</div>`;function plot(o){const m={l:o.yl?70:38,r:24,t:20,b:o.xl?54:30},sx=o.sx,sy=o.sy,W=o.x1*sx,H=o.y1*sy;const X=x=>m.l+x*sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*sy,gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${o.w||W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(0)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(0)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(0)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx)s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${Y(0)+18}" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${x}</text>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly)s+=`<text x="${m.l-7}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="13" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${y}</text>`;if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+46}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(18,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="${p.c||"var(--sea)"}" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-linejoin="round" ${p.dash?'stroke-dasharray="9 6"':""}/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="6" fill="${p[2]||"var(--coral)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.4"/>`});(o.labels||[]).forEach(l=>{s+=`<text x="${X(l[0])}" y="${Y(l[1])}" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="${l[2]||"start"}" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4">${l[3]!==void 0?l[3]:""}</text>`});return s+"</svg>"}function blueGraph(){return plot({alt:"Graph of kilometers traveled against hours of hauling for Team Blue. A straight line rises from the origin through 2 hours and 12 kilometers and 4 hours and 24 kilometers.",x1:6,y1:40,sx:66,sy:8.4,gy:5,ly:10,xl:"Hours of hauling",yl:"Distance traveled (km)",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[6,36]]}],pts:[[2,12],[4,24]],labels:[[.5,33,"start","Team Blue"]]})}function teamGraph(){return plot({alt:"Graph of distance from base camp in kilometers against days for two teams. Team One rises from the origin. Team Two falls from 24 kilometers at day zero to zero at day six. The two lines cross.",x1:6,y1:48,sx:64,sy:6.6,gy:4,ly:8,xl:"Days since Monday morning",yl:"Distance from base camp (km)",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[6,48]]},{pts:[[0,24],[6,0]],c:"var(--coral)",dash:true}],labels:[[.45,41,"start","Team One"],[3.75,14,"start","Team Two"]]})}const redTable=()=>bigTable(["Hours of hauling","2","4","6"],[["Distance traveled (km)","11","22","33"]],"Team Red’s log book");const ICE_TABLE=()=>bigTable(["Segment","South: towed","South: drill","East: towed","East: drill"],[["1","142","139","108","104"],["2","118","115","97","92"],["3","86","79","112","110"],["4","121","118","94","91"]],"Ice thickness in centimeters. Route South is 48 km long. Route East is 61 km long.");const EK_HEAD="Memo from Tomas Ek, operations officer, to the station committee. September 2026.";const EK=`<p>We should stop planting flag lines and navigate by GPS waypoints instead.</p>
<p>Planting a twelve kilometer line takes two people a full day and uses 120 bamboo poles, which have to be flown in and are not recovered until spring, if at all. A handheld receiver fixes a position to within three meters anywhere on the route, in any visibility, at no running cost.</p>
<p>Last season every sledge team that carried a receiver arrived at its depot. Three of our six teams carried one. That is a perfect record for the receivers.</p>
<p>The flag line is also a hazard in its own right. Last winter a sledge runner caught a buried pole and bent, and the repair cost four working hours.</p>
<p>Finally, nobody under thirty on this station has ever asked for flags.</p>`;const VARGA_HEAD="Memo from Dr. Lia Varga, field safety officer, to the station committee. September 2026.";const VARGA=`<p>The flag line should stay, and here is what the operations memo leaves out.</p>
<p>A receiver tells you where you are. It does not tell you whether the ground between you and the next waypoint will hold. Our flag lines are not planted along a straight bearing. They are planted along a route somebody has drilled and walked, which is why they curve.</p>
<p>Of the three teams that carried receivers last season, two followed the flag line anyway and one did not. The team that did not took four hours longer than planned and finished the leg on foot after a sledge runner dropped into a melt channel. One case is not a record.</p>
<p>Batteries fail at thirty below. Flags do not have batteries.</p>
<p>I agree with the operations memo about the bent runner. A buried pole is a real hazard, and the answer is to pull the line in autumn, not to stop planting it.</p>`;const memo=(head,body)=>`<article style="flex:1 1 330px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><div style="font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</div></article>`;const memoPair=()=>`<div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:12px">${memo(EK_HEAD,EK)}${memo(VARGA_HEAD,VARGA)}</div>`;const ekOnly=()=>`<div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:12px">${memo(EK_HEAD,EK)}</div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const funcTableP=()=>bigTable(["Hours hauled","1","2","3","4"],[["Kilometers from camp","6","12","18","24"]],"Table P: one sledge team's log");const funcTableQ=()=>bigTable(["Depth in meters","10","10","20","30"],[["Water temperature in degrees Celsius","−1.2","−1.6","−1.8","−1.9"]],"Table Q: readings from two casts on different days at the same hole");const SCAT=[[8,30],[12,36],[14,38],[15,41],[18,46],[20,50],[22,54],[25,59],[28,64],[31,70],[16,88]];function stoveScatter(){return plot({alt:"Scatter plot of stove minutes per person each day against degrees below zero. Ten points rise from left to right in a narrow band, and one point sits high above the band at 16 degrees below zero and 88 minutes.",x1:35,y1:90,sx:14,sy:3.4,gx:5,lx:5,gy:10,ly:10,xl:"Degrees below zero",yl:"Stove minutes per person each day",pts:SCAT})}function triGrid(){return F.coordGrid({max:10,quad4:true,size:400,points:[[1,1,"A"],[4,1,"B"],[4,3,"C"]],lines:[[[1,1],[4,1]],[[4,1],[4,3]],[[4,3],[1,1]]]})}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Depot Four",short:"Depot Four",parts:[{std:["RL.8.1"],dur:150,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence for what a story says and for inferences",html:story,qs:["What does the station rule say a team must do when it drops below two days of fuel? Read me the exact words that prove it.","Which one is the strongest evidence that reaching the drill site mattered a great deal to Dr. Oyelaran? Pick A, B, or C and tell why. A: “Twelve kilometers,” he said. B: “Three seasons of grant money had gone into getting a team to that spot. Dr. Oyelaran had written the proposal himself, twice, after the first one was turned down.” C: “He was on his knees in the snow with his glove off.”","The story never says why Mira pauses with her thumb on the radio button without pressing. What can you infer? Read me the words that most strongly support your inference.","How does Dr. Oyelaran feel about Mira’s call by the end of the story? Read me the one sentence that most strongly supports your answer."],key:["It must turn back. Reads “Any team below two days of fuel turns back. No exceptions.” (RL.8.1, explicit evidence)","B. It gives three seasons of grant money and a proposal written twice after a rejection, which shows years of effort. A is only the distance, and C shows he is worried at that moment but not how much the site mattered over time. (RL.8.1, strongest evidence)","She knows the call ends the trip and takes the drill site away from Dr. Oyelaran, so she hesitates. Strongest support: “Three seasons of grant money had gone into getting a team to that spot,” or his line “We would be at the site by dark.” (RL.8.1, inference)","He accepts it, or he agrees with it even though he is disappointed. Strongest evidence: “The site will still be there in November.” May also cite that he “took the front traces of the sled without a word.” (RL.8.1, inference)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Sledge Speeds",short:"Sled Speeds",parts:[{std:["8.EE.B.5"],dur:300,dir:"Hauling means pulling a loaded sled across the ice. Look at the graph and the log book. Answer out loud. Sketch number 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"slope as a unit rate and comparing proportional relationships shown as a graph, an equation, and a table",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four teams haul sleds away from base camp. Each team travels at a steady speed, so distance and time make a proportional relationship.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${blueGraph()}</div>${redTable()}`,qs:["The graph shows Team Blue. What is the slope of the line? What does the slope mean for this team?","Team Gold’s distance is given by the equation d = 7.5h, where d is kilometers and h is hours. Which team hauls faster, Blue or Gold? How do you know?","Look at Team Red’s log book. Is Team Red’s relationship proportional? What is its unit rate? Would Team Red’s line be steeper or less steep than Team Blue’s?","On your paper, sketch Team Gold’s line from 0 to 4 hours. Name two points that are on your line.","A fourth sled is towed by a snow tractor. Its distance is d = 9h. How far does it get in 3.5 hours? How long does it take to reach a depot 54 km away?","Every one of these graphs passes through the point (0, 0). Why? What would it mean if a team’s graph crossed the vertical axis at 4 instead of 0?"],key:["The slope is 6. Team Blue hauls 6 kilometers each hour. The slope is the unit rate. From the graph, (2, 12) and (4, 24) both give 12 ÷ 2 = 6 and 24 ÷ 4 = 6. Or: take the rise over the run between any two gridlines on the line. Partial: 6 given with no account of what it means for the team is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)","Team Gold. Gold’s unit rate is 7.5 kilometers per hour and Blue’s is 6 kilometers per hour, so Gold is faster. Compares a graph with an equation. Or: graph Gold on the same axes and compare the steepness. Partial: the right team with no two rates compared is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)","Yes, it is proportional: 11 ÷ 2 = 5.5, 22 ÷ 4 = 5.5, and 33 ÷ 6 = 5.5, so the ratio is constant and the line goes through (0, 0). The unit rate is 5.5 kilometers per hour. Less steep than Team Blue, because 5.5 is less than 6. Partial: two of the three parts answered is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)","A straight line through (0, 0) and (4, 30). Other correct points: (2, 15), (1, 7.5). Partial: a correct line with only one point named is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)","31.5 kilometers, because 9 × 3.5 = 31.5. It takes 6 hours, because 54 ÷ 9 = 6. Or: 9 × 3 = 27 plus half of 9, which is 31.5. Partial: one of the two answers right is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)","At 0 hours a team has traveled 0 kilometers, and a proportional relationship always passes through the origin. A line crossing at 4 would mean the team had already covered 4 kilometers before the timing started, so the relationship would not be proportional and distance ÷ time would not give the same number at every point. Partial: explaining the origin with nothing said about the line crossing at 4 is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)"]}]},{id:3,title:"The Line That Turns It",short:"Turning Line",parts:[{std:["RL.8.3"],dur:165,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the line or the sentence you are talking about.",desc:"explaining how incidents and lines of dialogue propel the action, reveal character, and provoke a decision",html:story,qs:["Finding the split fuel can at Depot Four is one incident. How does it push the story forward? What choice does it put in front of the team?","Mira answers Dr. Oyelaran’s plan with one question: “And then what do we drink?” How does that line change the moment? What does it reveal about Mira?","The story says Dr. Oyelaran was “on his knees in the snow with his glove off, which he never did.” What does that detail reveal about him right then?","His last line is “The site will still be there in November.” What does that line reveal about how he has changed since the start of the scene?"],key:["The depot was supposed to refill their fuel, and instead it leaves them with about one day. That turns a routine pickup into a decision, because the station rule says a team below two days of fuel turns back. The choice is to push the twelve kilometers to the drill site or to turn for home, and everything after it follows from that choice. (RL.8.3, an incident propels the action)","It names the thing his plan leaves out, that fuel is what melts snow into drinking water, so his argument stops: “He did not answer.” It reveals that Mira thinks about what the team needs to survive and that she will push back on a senior scientist instead of going along. (RL.8.3, dialogue propels the action and reveals character)","He is alarmed and fully focused. Taking a glove off in that cold is dangerous and he never does it, so breaking his own habit shows he needs to see and feel the damaged crate for himself and how much the trip to the site matters to him. (RL.8.3, an incident reveals character)","He has let go of pushing for the site. At the start he argues for the twelve kilometers, and at the end he treats the site as something that can wait, which shows he accepts the rule and respects Mira’s call. (RL.8.3, dialogue reveals character)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Water Source Check",short:"Water Check",parts:[{std:["W.8.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:150,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Do numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"research skills (judging sources, choosing search terms, fixing plagiarism, and citing)",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>The two sources disagree about where a camp’s water comes from. Which source would you trust? Give two reasons from the source information.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: snow. B: polar field camp melt snow drinking water fuel. C: cold places to visit.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student’s report says: <em>Melting and warming enough snow for one person for one day burns about 0.4 liters of kerosene.</em> There are no quotation marks and no source named. Fix it on your paper. Quote it correctly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write a citation for Source A with the author, title, journal, and date.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons, such as: it names an author with a job in polar logistics, it comes from a field journal, it carries a date, and it gives an exact figure. Source B is an undated forum post by an unknown user who writes “I read somewhere” and “Pretty sure.” Full credit for two reasons drawn from the source lines. For the teacher: Source B is also wrong on the facts, because boiling sea water leaves the salt behind rather than removing it. (W.8.8, credibility and accuracy)","B. It uses the key words from the research question, so the results will be about melting snow for water at a polar camp. A is far too broad and C is off topic. (W.8.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Dr. Hana Lindqvist writes, “Melting and warming enough snow for one person for one day burns about 0.4 liters of kerosene.” Paraphrase: According to Dr. Hana Lindqvist, a camp spends roughly four tenths of a liter of stove fuel per person each day just to turn snow into water. The student must name the source either way. (W.8.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","Has all four parts. Example: Lindqvist, Hana. “Water from Snow.” Polar Field Quarterly, March 2026. Any clear, consistent order is fine. (W.8.8, citation)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Two Teams, One Ice",short:"Two Teams",parts:[{std:["8.EE.C.8"],dur:300,dir:"Use the graph for number 1. Solve the rest on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"solving pairs of simultaneous linear equations by graph and by algebra, including word problems and the number of solutions",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two teams are out on the ice at the same time. Team One leaves base camp on Monday morning and hauls away from camp. Team Two starts at a depot far out on the ice and hauls back toward camp.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${teamGraph()}</div>`,qs:["At what point do the two lines cross? What does that point mean for the two teams?","Solve this pair of equations with algebra: y = 2x + 5 and y = −3x − 10.","Solve this pair of equations with algebra: x + y = 34 and x − y = 8.","Without solving, tell how many solutions each pair has and how you know. (a) y = 3x + 7 and y = 3x − 2. (b) x + 2y = 6 and 3x + 6y = 18.","A sled carries 18 ration crates in all. A small crate holds 12 kg of food and a large crate holds 20 kg. The crates hold 280 kg of food together. Write a pair of equations and find how many crates are small and how many are large.","Sledge One leaves camp at 8 a.m. and covers 5 km each hour. Sledge Two leaves the same camp at 11 a.m. on the same route and covers 8 km each hour. Write a pair of equations and find when Sledge Two catches up, and how far from camp that happens."],key:["(2, 16). After 2 days both teams are 16 kilometers from base camp, so that is when and where they meet on the route. Partial: the crossing point read right with no account of what it means is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","x = −3 and y = −1, or (−3, −1). Steps: 2x + 5 = −3x − 10, so 5x = −15 and x = −3, then y = 2(−3) + 5 = −1. Check in the other equation: −3(−3) − 10 = 9 − 10 = −1. Or: substitution, elimination, or graphing both lines all count. Partial: x = −3 with no y is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","x = 21 and y = 13, or (21, 13). Steps: add the two equations to get 2x = 42, so x = 21, then 21 + y = 34 gives y = 13. Check: 21 − 13 = 8. Or: substitution, y = 34 − x into x − y = 8, reaches the same pair. Partial: x = 21 with no y is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","(a) No solution. Both slopes are 3 and the y-intercepts are different, so the lines are parallel and never cross. Substituting gives 3x + 7 = 3x − 2, which says 7 = −2 and is never true. (b) Infinitely many solutions. Multiplying the first equation by 3 gives 3x + 6y = 18, which is the second equation, so they are the same line. For example (2, 2) works in both. Partial: one of the two pairs answered is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","10 small crates and 8 large crates. Equations: s + l = 18 and 12s + 20l = 280. Steps: s = 18 − l, so 12(18 − l) + 20l = 280, which gives 216 + 8l = 280, so 8l = 64 and l = 8, then s = 10. Check: 10 + 8 = 18 and 120 + 160 = 280. Or: elimination, multiplying the first equation by 12 and subtracting, or guess and check against both conditions. Partial: a correct pair of equations with the solving unfinished is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","Sledge Two catches up 8 hours after 8 a.m., which is 4 p.m., 40 kilometers from camp. Equations with t as hours after 8 a.m.: d = 5t and d = 8(t − 3). Steps: 5t = 8t − 24, so 3t = 24 and t = 8, then d = 5(8) = 40. Check: 8(8 − 3) = 8(5) = 40. Or: build a table of the two distances hour by hour and look for the row where they match. Partial: the equations right with the time or the distance missing is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Field Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"informative",label:"Explain",icon:"📰",hint:"How a field team stays warm and dry"},std:["W.8.2"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:"Write on your paper for 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an informative writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>`<p class="big">A new field assistant is flying out to your camp next week and has never worked in deep cold. Write to explain how a polar field team stays warm and dry. Use the fact box. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: staying warm and dry in deep cold",["Very cold air holds almost no moisture, so sweat is the main danger. Damp clothing stops holding heat.","Teams dress in layers. You take a layer off before hard work and put it back on the moment you stop.","A tent is pitched with its door away from the wind, and blocks of snow are cut and stacked as a wall on the windward side.","Boots go inside the sleeping bag at night, because wet boots left out freeze stiff by morning.","A stove is run only in the tent porch. A stove inside the sleeping space makes steam that freezes on the walls and rains down later.","Frostbite starts at fingers, toes, nose, and ears, so partners check each other’s faces every hour while they are moving."])}${checklist(["Introduce the topic and tell the reader what you will cover","Group the information in an order that makes sense, such as clothing, then camp, then checks","Explain the facts in your own sentences, and define any term the reader would not know","Use transitions: first, after that, because, as a result, however","Use topic words: layers, windward, porch, frostbite","Keep a formal style, with no slang","End with a conclusion"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and previews what the piece will cover (W.8.2)","Organizes information into groups or categories in a logical order (W.8.2)","Develops the topic with relevant facts and details from the fact box, such as taking a layer off before hard work or keeping the stove in the porch (W.8.2)","Defines or explains at least one technical term for a reader who has never been in deep cold, such as windward or frostbite (W.8.2)","Uses varied transitions to connect ideas, such as first, after that, because, as a result (W.8.2)","Uses precise language and topic vocabulary and keeps a formal style (W.8.2)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from the explanation (W.8.2)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"narrative",label:"Story",icon:"📖",hint:"A late radio call at base camp"},std:["W.8.3"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:"Write on your paper for 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You are the radio operator at a polar base camp. At nine in the evening a call comes in from a sledge team that is not where it is supposed to be. Write the story of the next hour. Write 10 to 12 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Set the scene: where you are, who is telling the story, and what you can see and hear","Tell the events in the order they happen","Include at least two lines of dialogue, including part of the radio call","Use details the reader can see, hear, or feel","Use time words: at first, a few minutes later, by the time, finally","End by showing what the narrator felt or understood by the end of the hour"])}`,key:["Establishes a situation and a point of view and introduces the narrator or other characters (W.8.3)","Events unfold in an order that is natural and logical (W.8.3)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing, description, or reflection (W.8.3)","Includes at least two lines of dialogue that move the story along (W.8.3)","Uses transition words to show sequence and shifts in time or place (W.8.3)","Uses precise words, descriptive details, and sensory language (W.8.3)","Ends with a conclusion that follows from and reflects on the events (W.8.3)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Reading the Ice",short:"Ice Thickness",parts:[{std:["RI.8.1"],dur:150,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence from a science article for explicit answers and for inferences",html:iceText,qs:["When the two methods disagree by more than ten centimeters, which number goes in the record book? Read me the exact words that prove it.","The article says speed comes with a cost. Which one sentence most strongly supports the idea that the towed instrument can miss something that matters? Read it to me.","Claim: the towed instrument lets a team cover far more ground than the drill. Find the two sentences that, together, most strongly support this claim.","A team has two days left. It needs a thickness map of a wide area and it also needs exact numbers at the few spots that look risky. What would the team most likely do? Read me the words that most strongly support your inference."],key:["The drill number. Reads “Where the two methods disagree by more than ten centimeters, the drill number is the one that goes in the record book.” (RI.8.1, explicit evidence)","“It smooths over a narrow crack or a pressure ridge, which is a wall of broken ice pushed up where two floes have collided.” Weaker choices: that it reports an average over several meters, which is true but does not say what gets missed. Full credit for the smoothing sentence. (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","“One line of forty holes takes a team most of a day in air that can sit at thirty degrees below zero.” and “A sled crew can read a thirty kilometer line in an afternoon.” Both are needed, because the claim is a comparison and each sentence supplies one side of it. (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","Use both methods: tow the instrument along the route first, then drill a short line of check holes at the spots that look risky. Strongest support: “A sled crew runs the instrument along a route, and then the field team drills a short line of check holes wherever the instrument readings change fast.” (RI.8.1, inference)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Verb Drift",short:"Verbs",parts:[{std:["L.8.1"],dur:140,dir:"A verbal is a verb form doing another job. Answer out loud. When you fix a sentence, say the whole sentence the correct way.",desc:"verbals, active and passive voice, subjunctive mood, and fixing a shift in voice",qs:["“Packing the sleds took the crew three hours.” Which word is the verbal? What kind is it, and what job does it do in the sentence?","“The radio operator, listening for the weather call, wrote nothing down.” Find the verbal and name its kind. Who or what does it describe?","“The plan was to reach the depot before dark.” Find the verbal and name its kind.","Say this sentence in active voice: “The supply crates were lashed to the sled by two field assistants.”","Fix the verb: “I wish the storm was over.”","Fix the shift in voice: “The cook melted snow for water, and then the stove was refilled by her.”"],key:["Packing. Gerund. With its phrase, “Packing the sleds,” it is the subject of the sentence, so it acts as a noun. (L.8.1, verbals)","listening. Participle. It describes the radio operator, so it acts as an adjective. (L.8.1, verbals)","to reach. Infinitive. (L.8.1, verbals)","Two field assistants lashed the supply crates to the sled. (L.8.1, active and passive voice)","I wish the storm were over. A wish takes were, not was. (L.8.1, subjunctive mood)","The cook melted snow for water and then refilled the stove. Both verbs are now active. (L.8.1, shift in voice)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Radio the Base",short:"Radio",parts:[{std:["SL.8.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:150,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken briefing that presents a claim and findings with evidence from a data table",html:()=>`<p class="big">A loaded snow tractor convoy must cross the frozen sea to the far camp. The ice has to be at least 90 centimeters thick the whole way. Your survey measured four segments of each route two ways: a towed instrument and a drill. When the two numbers differ, the team uses the lower one.</p>${ICE_TABLE()}<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Radio a briefing to base camp. Say which route the convoy should take, give your strongest evidence from the table, name the drawback of your choice, and end with what you need base camp to do.</p><div class="row center" style="margin-top:16px;gap:14px">${["My recommendation","The evidence","The drawback","What I need from base"].map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`,key:["States a clear recommendation up front and stays focused on it. Route East is the supportable choice: every segment is at or above 91 cm once the lower number is used. A student who recommends Route South must add a detour or a second survey at segment 3. (SL.8.4)","Emphasizes the most important points in a focused, coherent order rather than reading the whole table out (SL.8.4)","Backs the recommendation with relevant evidence and sound reasoning from the table, such as Route South segment 3 measuring 79 cm by drill, which is 11 cm under the 90 cm limit (SL.8.4)","Uses well-chosen details, including the drawback: Route East is 61 km instead of 48 km, so it is 13 km longer and burns more fuel and more days (SL.8.4)","Appropriate eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation (SL.8.4)"]}]},{id:10,title:"The Shape of a Hand",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.8.4"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look back at the story. Then answer out loud.",desc:"explaining figurative and connotative word choices and their effect on meaning and tone",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Three lines from Depot Four.</p>${checklist(["“the kerosene had drained into the snow and frozen there in a brown stain the shape of a hand”","“a line of black flags ran back toward the station like stitches in cloth”","“The wind had begun to move the loose snow in long ribbons across the surface, which is the way it starts.”"])}${lookBack("Look back at the story",story())}`,qs:["Why describe spilled fuel as a brown stain the shape of a hand? What does that image do to the tone of the scene?","The flag line is compared to stitches in cloth. What does that comparison suggest about the flags, and why does it belong in this paragraph in particular?","The last line says “which is the way it starts,” without saying what starts. What is starting, and what does leaving it unnamed do to the reader?"],key:["A hand-shaped stain makes the loss look deliberate, almost like something reached out and took the fuel, and a hand is a human shape in a place with nobody in it. The tone turns from a routine stop into something still and uneasy. Saying “a brown patch” would have reported the same fact with none of that. (RL.8.4, figurative meaning and tone)","Stitches hold two pieces of cloth together, so the comparison makes the flag line the thing holding the team to the station. It comes in the paragraph where Mira is about to key the radio and end the trip, so the image tells the reader what she is choosing: the line back, not the twelve kilometers forward. (RL.8.4, impact of word choice on meaning)","The wind is starting to rise into the kind of weather that traps a team. Not naming it lets the narration stay inside the voice of someone who already knows what that looks like and does not need to spell it out, which is more unsettling than a sentence explaining the danger. It also keeps the pressure on the decision without turning the scene into a rescue. (RL.8.4, connotation and tone)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Flags or Waypoints",short:"The Argument",parts:[{std:["RI.8.8"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the operations memo. Then answer out loud.",desc:"delineating and evaluating an argument, including unsound reasoning and irrelevant evidence",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The station committee is deciding whether to keep planting flag lines.</p>${ekOnly()}`,qs:["What is Ek's claim, and what reasons does he give for it?","One of his pieces of evidence does not support the claim at all. Which one, and why not?","One of his reasons looks like strong evidence but the reasoning is not sound. Which one, and what is wrong with it?"],key:["Claim: the station should stop planting flag lines and navigate by GPS waypoints instead. Reasons: planting a line is slow and costs poles that are not recovered; a receiver fixes a position to three meters in any visibility at no running cost; the receivers have a perfect arrival record; and a buried pole bent a sledge runner, so the line is itself a hazard. Needs the claim and at least two reasons. (RI.8.8, delineate the argument)","The last line, that nobody under thirty on the station has ever asked for flags. What people have asked for says nothing about whether a route is safe to cross, and the age of the person not asking says less still. It is irrelevant evidence. (RI.8.8, irrelevant evidence)","The perfect record. Only three of six teams carried a receiver, so the sample is half the teams and the memo does not say what happened to the three without one. Calling three arrivals a perfect record makes a very small number sound like proof. Arriving also does not show that the receiver is what got them there. Accept instead the cost reason, if the student notes that it prices the flags but never prices the thing the flags do. (RI.8.8, insufficient evidence and unsound reasoning)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Where They Disagree",short:"Two Memos",parts:[{std:["RI.8.9"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read both memos. Then answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing two texts that give conflicting information and locating the disagreements",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Two officers wrote to the same committee in the same month.</p>${memoPair()}`,qs:["Find a point of fact where the two memos conflict. What does each one say?","Find a place where they agree on a fact but read it in opposite ways.","A committee member reads only the first memo. What would that reader believe that the second memo would change?"],key:["The record of the teams carrying receivers. Ek writes that every sledge team that carried a receiver arrived at its depot and calls it a perfect record. Varga writes that of the three, two followed the flag line anyway and one did not, and the one that did not took four hours longer and finished the leg on foot after a runner dropped into a melt channel. The two accounts of the same three teams cannot both be complete. (RI.8.9, conflict on a matter of fact)","The bent sledge runner. Both agree it happened and that a buried pole caused it. Ek reads it as evidence that the flag line is a hazard and should go. Varga agrees it is a real hazard and reads it as evidence that the line should be pulled in autumn. The same fact supports opposite conclusions depending on what each writer thinks the problem is. (RI.8.9, conflict of interpretation)","That a receiver does the same job as a flag line, only faster and cheaper, and that the three arrivals prove it. Varga's memo changes that in two ways: a receiver reports a position but not whether the ice will hold, and the flag line curves because it follows a route somebody drilled and walked. It also removes the perfect record by telling what actually happened to the third team, and it adds that batteries fail at thirty below. (RI.8.9, what each text leaves the reader believing)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Write to the Committee",short:"Argument",parts:[{std:["W.8.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an argument with a claim, counterclaim, relevant evidence, and a conclusion",html:()=>`<p class="big">You are on the station committee. Write an argument for what the station should do about route marking next season. Use evidence from both memos. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Open with a clear claim","Acknowledge what the other side claims, and answer it","Support your claim with relevant evidence from the memos","Use words and phrases that link your claim, reasons, and evidence","Keep a formal style, with no slang","End with a conclusion that follows from your argument"])}${lookBack("Look back at the two memos",memoPair())}`,key:["Introduces a clear claim and organizes the reasons and evidence in an order that makes sense (W.8.1, claim and organization)","Acknowledges the opposing claim and answers it rather than ignoring it (W.8.1, counterclaim)","Supports the claim with relevant evidence from the memos, such as the 120 poles and the full day of work, the three meter fix in any visibility, the curve of a drilled route, the melt channel, or batteries failing at thirty below (W.8.1, relevant evidence)","Uses words, phrases, or clauses to link claim, reasons, and evidence, and keeps a formal style (W.8.1, cohesion and style)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from the argument (W.8.1, conclusion)","A claim of either kind can earn full marks. What is being rated is whether the evidence is relevant and whether the other side is answered."]}]},{id:14,title:"Numbers That Do Not End",short:"Rationals",parts:[{std:["8.NS.A.1"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up and say each answer.",desc:"telling rational numbers from irrational ones and converting a repeating decimal to a fraction",qs:["Sort these into rational and irrational: 0.75, the square root of 49, the square root of 50, 0.3333 repeating, and pi.","Write 0.4444 repeating as a fraction. Show the steps.","The square root of 50 lies between which two whole numbers? Which of the two is it closer to, and how do you know?"],key:["Rational: 0.75, which is 3/4; the square root of 49, which is 7; and 0.3333 repeating, which is 1/3. Irrational: the square root of 50 and pi. A number is rational when it can be written as one whole number over another, and its decimal either stops or repeats. Partial: the five sorted right with no account of how is approaching, and so is one misplaced number with the reasoning sound. (8.NS.A.1)","4/9. Let x = 0.4444 repeating. Then 10x = 4.4444 repeating. Subtracting gives 9x = 4, so x = 4/9. Check: 4 divided by 9 is 0.4444 repeating. Or: name it from the pattern, since 0.1111 repeating is 1/9. Partial: 4/9 with no steps shown is approaching. (8.NS.A.1, converting a repeating decimal)","Between 7 and 8, because 7 squared is 49 and 8 squared is 64. It is much closer to 7, because 50 is only 1 above 49 and 14 below 64. The square root of 50 is about 7.07. Partial: the two whole numbers right with no answer about which is nearer is approaching. (8.NS.A.1, approximating an irrational number)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Powers at the Depot",short:"Exponents",parts:[{std:["8.EE.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up and say each answer.",desc:"applying the properties of integer exponents",qs:["Write 3 squared times 3 to the power negative 5 as a single power, then as a fraction.","Simplify 2 cubed, all squared, divided by 2 to the fourth.","Explain why 5 to the power 0 equals 1, using the rule for dividing powers."],key:["3 to the power negative 3, which is 1/27. Adding the exponents gives 2 + (−5) = −3, and a negative exponent means one over the power: 1 over 3 cubed. Or: 9 ÷ 243 = 1/27 worked out as numbers. Partial: the single power right with no fraction is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)","4. Raising a power to a power multiplies the exponents, so 2 cubed all squared is 2 to the sixth. Dividing subtracts: 6 − 4 = 2, so the answer is 2 squared, which is 4. Or: 8 squared is 64, and 64 ÷ 16 = 4 worked out as numbers. (8.EE.A.1)","5 cubed divided by 5 cubed is 1, because any number other than zero divided by itself is 1. By the rule for dividing powers it is also 5 to the power 3 − 3, which is 5 to the power 0. Both are the same quantity, so 5 to the power 0 must be 1. Or: step down the powers, 125, 25, 5, and each step divides by 5, so after 5 comes 1. (8.EE.A.1)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Roots and Sides",short:"Roots",parts:[{std:["8.EE.A.2"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up and say each answer.",desc:"using square root and cube root symbols to solve equations and evaluating roots",qs:["Solve x squared equals 169.","Solve x cubed equals 64. Then evaluate the square root of 81 and the cube root of 27.","Why does x squared equals 50 have no whole number solution? Give an estimate of x, and say whether that value is rational or irrational."],key:["x = 13 or x = −13. Both work, because a negative times a negative is positive. The symbol for the square root of 169 means only the positive one, 13, so the equation has the two solutions while the symbol names one. Partial: x = 13 only leaves the negative root out and is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","x = 4, since 4 cubed is 64. The square root of 81 is 9 and the cube root of 27 is 3. Partial: two of the three right is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","50 is not a perfect square. It sits between 49 and 64, so x is between 7 and 8, about 7.07 or about −7.07. The square root of 50 is irrational, because its decimal never ends and never repeats. Partial: the estimate right with no answer about rational or irrational is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)"]}]},{id:17,title:"One Output Each",short:"Functions",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.1"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the two tables. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding whether a rule is a function and describing the graph of a function as ordered pairs",fig:()=>`${funcTableP()}${funcTableQ()}`,qs:["Is Table P a function of the hours hauled? Use the definition in your answer.","Is Table Q a function of the depth? Use the definition in your answer.","Table P is graphed. What does one point on that graph stand for, and how could you tell from a graph alone whether a rule is a function?"],key:["Yes. A function assigns each input exactly one output. Every number of hours in Table P appears once and has one distance, so each input has exactly one output. Partial: yes with no use of the definition is approaching. (8.F.A.1)","No. The input 10 meters appears twice, with −1.2 and with −1.6 degrees. One input has two different outputs, so depth alone does not determine the temperature in this table. The readings came from two different casts, so the day is doing some of the work. Partial: no with no account of the repeated input is approaching. (8.F.A.1)","Each point is one ordered pair, an input with the output that goes with it, such as (3, 18) meaning 18 kilometers after 3 hours. The graph of a function is the whole set of those pairs. On a graph you can check by looking for any two points stacked directly above each other: that would be one input with two outputs, so the rule would not be a function. Or: naming the vertical line test counts for the second half. Partial: one of the two halves answered is approaching. (8.F.A.1)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Straight or Not",short:"Linear",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.3"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up and say each answer.",desc:"interpreting y = mx + b as a linear function and giving an example of a function that is not linear",qs:["The fuel left in the camp tank is given by f = 48 − 6d, where f is liters and d is days the stove has run. Is this a linear function? Name m and b and say what each one means at camp.","The area of a square snow block of side s is A = s squared. Is that a linear function? Give three points and use them to show your answer.","On the Two Teams graph, Team Two starts 24 kilometers from camp and comes back 4 kilometers a day. Write its rule in the form y = mx + b. Is it proportional?"],key:["Yes, it is linear, because it has the form y = mx + b. Here m = −6, which means the stove burns 6 liters a day, and b = 48, which is the 48 liters in the tank before the stove is lit. The graph is a straight line falling 6 for every 1 across. Partial: yes with one of m and b not explained is approaching. (8.F.A.3)","No. Three points are (1, 1), (2, 4) and (3, 9). From the first to the second the area rises by 3, and from the second to the third it rises by 5, so the rise is not the same for each step of 1 and the three points are not on one straight line. Or: sketch it and show the graph curves. Partial: no with points given but no comparison of the steps is approaching. (8.F.A.3, a function that is not linear)","y = −4x + 24, where x is days and y is kilometers from camp. It is not proportional, because b is 24 rather than 0, so the line does not pass through the origin. A proportional relationship has the form y = mx with nothing added. Partial: the rule right with no answer about proportionality is approaching. (8.F.A.3)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Move the Triangle",short:"Transformations",parts:[{std:["8.G.A.3"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"Look at the grid. Work on your paper. Hold it up and say the coordinates.",desc:"describing the effect of a translation, a reflection, a rotation, and a dilation using coordinates",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${triGrid()}</div>`,qs:["Translate the triangle 3 units right and 2 units up. Give the new coordinates of A, B and C.","Reflect the original triangle across the x-axis. Give the new coordinates, and say what the reflection does to each coordinate.","Rotate the original triangle 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin. Give the rule in the form (x, y) goes to (___, ___), and give the new coordinates.","Dilate the original triangle from the origin by a scale factor of 2. Give the new coordinates. What happens to the side lengths, and what happens to the angles?"],key:["A goes to (4, 3), B to (7, 3) and C to (7, 5). A translation adds the same amount to every x and the same amount to every y, here 3 and 2. Or: count the squares on the grid instead of adding. Partial: two of the three points right is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","A goes to (1, −1), B to (4, −1) and C to (4, −3). A reflection across the x-axis keeps each x and changes the sign of each y. Partial: the three points right with nothing said about the coordinates is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","The rule is (x, y) goes to (−y, x). A(1, 1) goes to (−1, 1), B(4, 1) goes to (−1, 4) and C(4, 3) goes to (−3, 4). Check one: B is (4, 1), so the image is (−1, 4). A rotation keeps every side length and every angle, so the image is congruent to the original. Or: trace the triangle and turn the paper a quarter turn to the left, or call it a 270 degree clockwise turn. Partial: the rule right with a point wrong, or three right points with no rule, is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","A goes to (2, 2), B to (8, 2) and C to (8, 6). A dilation from the origin multiplies both coordinates by the scale factor. Every side length is doubled, so AB goes from 3 to 6 and BC from 2 to 4, but every angle stays exactly the same, so the new triangle is the same shape at twice the size. Partial: the points and the side lengths right with nothing said about the angles is approaching. (8.G.A.3)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Straight Line Home",short:"Pythagoras",parts:[{std:["8.G.B.7"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up and say each answer with its units.",desc:"applying the Pythagorean theorem to find unknown side lengths in real-world problems",qs:["A sledge team hauls 12 kilometers due east, then 9 kilometers due north. How far is it from camp in a straight line?","A guy line runs from the top of a mast 2.4 meters tall to a peg in the ice 1.8 meters from the foot of the mast. How long is the guy line?","A right triangle has a hypotenuse of 26 meters and one leg of 10 meters. Find the other leg."],key:["15 kilometers. The two legs are 12 and 9, so 12 squared plus 9 squared is 144 + 81 = 225, and the square root of 225 is 15. Or: recognize the 3, 4, 5 triple scaled by 3. Partial: 225 found with no square root taken is approaching. (8.G.B.7)","3 meters. 2.4 squared is 5.76 and 1.8 squared is 3.24, and 5.76 + 3.24 = 9, whose square root is 3. The mast stands at a right angle to the ice, so the mast and the ground are the two legs and the guy line is the hypotenuse. Or: scale the 3, 4, 5 triple by 0.6, since 1.8 and 2.4 are 0.6 times 3 and 4. (8.G.B.7)","24 meters. 26 squared is 676 and 10 squared is 100, so the other leg squared is 676 − 100 = 576, and the square root of 576 is 24. Here the theorem is used backwards, subtracting rather than adding, because the missing side is a leg and not the hypotenuse. Or: recognize the 5, 12, 13 triple scaled by 2. Partial: adding instead of subtracting, giving √776, does not count. (8.G.B.7)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Cold and the Stove",short:"Scatter Plot",parts:[{std:["8.SP.A.1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the scatter plot. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting a scatter plot: association, clustering, an outlier, and the rate of change",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">For eleven days the camp recorded how cold it was and how long the stove ran for each person.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${stoveScatter()}</div>`,qs:["Describe the association between the two quantities. Is it positive or negative, is it linear, and how tightly do the points cluster?","One point does not belong to the pattern. Give its coordinates and suggest what could explain it.","Leave that point out. Using two points from the band, about how many extra stove minutes does each extra degree below zero cost?"],key:["A positive association: as the number of degrees below zero goes up, the stove minutes go up too. It is close to linear, because ten of the eleven points lie in a narrow band that is nearly straight. The clustering is tight, with the points rising steadily rather than scattering. Partial: two of the three parts answered is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, pattern of association)","The point at about (16, 88). It sits roughly 46 minutes above where the band would put a day that is 16 degrees below zero. An outlier like that has a reason behind it: the stove may have been serviced or running badly that day, a pot may have been melted for the whole team, or the record may be a mistake. Partial: the point named with no possible reason is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, outlier)","About 1.7 minutes for each extra degree. Using (8, 30) and (31, 70), the rise is 70 − 30 = 40 minutes over a run of 31 − 8 = 23 degrees, and 40 divided by 23 is about 1.7. Any pair of points from the band that gives roughly 1.6 to 1.8 is correct. Or: draw a trend line by eye and read its rise over one degree. Partial: a rise and a run found with no division is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, interpreting the trend)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Reading the Record",short:"Founding",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining why British colonial policy changed after 1763 and how colonists read the change",html:()=>`<p class="big">Britain and France fought a war in North America that ended in 1763. Britain won and its national debt roughly doubled. These measures followed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Measure</th><th>What it did</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1763</td><td>Proclamation Line</td><td>barred colonial settlement west of the Appalachians</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1764</td><td>Sugar Act</td><td>taxed molasses and sugar and tightened enforcement against smuggling</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1765</td><td>Quartering Act</td><td>required colonies to house and supply British troops</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1765</td><td>Stamp Act</td><td>taxed paper goods, newspapers, contracts and playing cards</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("compass",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["From London's point of view, what made all four of these reasonable after 1763?","Colonists had fought in that war expecting the land beyond the mountains. Why would the 1763 line infuriate them in particular?","The Stamp Act taxed newspapers, contracts and legal documents. Which colonists would that annoy most, and why did that choice turn out badly for Britain?"],key:["A doubled debt, a much larger empire to defend, and a standing army now stationed in North America. London reasoned that the colonies had been the chief beneficiaries of the war, had been lightly taxed for decades, and should pay a share of their own defense and stop smuggling. Partial: naming the debt with nothing said about London's reasoning is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","Because the western land was what many of them thought they had been fighting for, and some colonies' charters already claimed it. The line also protected Native nations' territory, which London found cheaper than frontier war, so colonists read it as their own government choosing other interests over theirs. Partial: naming the land with nothing said about whose interests London was choosing is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","Printers, lawyers, merchants and tavern keepers: the people who wrote, argued and distributed public opinion for a living. Taxing exactly those occupations gave the most articulate and best connected colonists a direct grievance, and they answered in print, in court and in organized boycotts. It is hard to think of a worse group to tax first. Partial: naming the occupations with nothing said about why taxing them was a mistake is approaching. (HSS-8.1)"]},{std:["HSS-8.2"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing federalism and sorting powers between the national government and the states",html:()=>`<p class="big">How the Constitution divides power.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>National government only</th><th>Both</th><th>States only</th></tr>
     <tr><td>coin money</td><td>tax</td><td>run schools</td></tr>
     <tr><td>declare war</td><td>build roads</td><td>issue marriage and driver licenses</td></tr>
     <tr><td>make treaties</td><td>make and enforce laws</td><td>run elections</td></tr>
     <tr><td>regulate commerce between states</td><td>set up courts</td><td>establish local governments</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The Tenth Amendment says powers not given to the national government and not denied to the states are kept by the states or the people. Article VI says the Constitution and national laws made under it are the supreme law of the land.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}${ico("books",74)}</div>`,qs:["A state passes a law that conflicts with a valid act of Congress. Which one governs, and which clause settles it?","Why would the framers put coining money and making treaties on the national list only?","Elections are run by the states, yet the Fifteenth, Nineteenth and Twenty Sixth Amendments all limit what states may do about voting. Explain how both are true."],key:["The act of Congress governs, under the supremacy clause in Article VI, provided Congress was acting within its constitutional powers. If it was not, the state law stands and the Tenth Amendment is the state's argument. Partial: naming the supremacy clause with no condition about Congress acting within its powers is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","Because both must speak for the whole country to work at all. Thirteen currencies would make interstate trade a constant exchange problem, and thirteen separate foreign policies would let one state commit or embarrass all the others. These are the powers where division produces chaos rather than local variety. Partial: one of the two powers explained is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","States still conduct elections, set polling places, print ballots and keep the rolls, but amendments to the Constitution put particular grounds for denying the vote out of their reach entirely. The power to administer is not the power to decide who counts as a voter, and each of those amendments removed one more basis for exclusion. Partial: one of the states' remaining power and the amendments' limit named is approaching. (HSS-8.2)"]},{std:["HSS-8.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a bill through Congress and identifying where citizens can act",html:()=>`<p class="big">The path of a bill, in order.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>A member introduces the bill</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>A committee holds hearings, amends it, and may let it die</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>The full chamber debates and votes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>The other chamber does steps 2 and 3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>5</td><td>Differences between the two versions are reconciled</td></tr>
     <tr><td>6</td><td>The president signs or vetoes</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Most bills die at step 2. Why does so much power sit with a committee?","Name the step where a citizen can give evidence directly, and one step where contacting your own representative is the most useful thing you can do.","A citizen who loses at step 6 has not run out of options. Name two things still available."],key:["Because thousands of bills are introduced and no chamber could debate them all, so committees are where the actual reading, expert testimony and rewriting happen. A committee chair who declines to schedule a bill can end it without any vote being recorded, which is real power exercised quietly. Partial: naming the workload with nothing said about a chair being able to end a bill quietly is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Step 2, where committees take public testimony and written comment. Contacting your own representative matters most at steps 3 and 4, when that member's recorded vote is about to be cast and constituent pressure counts. Accept step 2 as well, if the representative sits on the committee. Partial: one of the two steps identified is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Any two: press Congress to override the veto with two thirds of each chamber, work to elect members or a president who will pass it next session, pursue the same goal at the state level, organize public opinion and media attention, or challenge a related law in court. Losing a vote ends one attempt, not the issue. Partial: one next step named is approaching, and saying nothing more can be done is approaching too. (HSS-8.3)"]}]},{id:23,title:"No Inherited Rank",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the rejection of inherited rank as an ideal of the new nation",html:()=>`<p class="big">Britain had an aristocracy: titles passed from father to son, and estates passed whole to the eldest son so a family's land stayed intact for generations. The new United States dismantled that machinery.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Change</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
     <tr><td>The Constitution forbids the United States or any state to grant a title of nobility</td><td>no legal aristocracy can be created</td></tr>
     <tr><td>States abolished primogeniture, the rule giving the whole estate to the eldest son</td><td>land was divided among heirs and large holdings broke up over generations</td></tr>
     <tr><td>States abolished entail, which had kept an estate from ever being sold</td><td>land became ordinary property that could be bought and sold</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Offices and professions were open without regard to birth</td><td>a person's standing came from what they did</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("star",74)}${ico("key",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain how abolishing primogeniture would, over three or four generations, change who owned land.","Why would a country that had just fought a king bother to ban titles of nobility, since it had no nobles to begin with?","This ideal was real and it was partial. Name one way wealth and advantage still passed from parent to child."],key:["Dividing each estate among all the heirs means a large holding is cut into smaller and smaller pieces every generation, so no family can hold a great block of land indefinitely. The intended effect was to prevent a permanent landed class, and over time it did spread ownership more widely in the states that adopted it. Partial: describing the division with nothing said about the intended effect is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","To prevent one from forming. The framers had watched wealthy families in several states acquire something close to hereditary standing, and a title is the legal device that makes such a position permanent and unanswerable. Banning the device was cheap insurance against an outcome they had just fought a war to avoid. Partial: naming the ban with nothing said about what a title would make permanent is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","Any accurate answer: inherited money, land and enslaved people still passed down and were the largest source of wealth; access to schooling, apprenticeships and connections ran through families; and the law excluded enslaved people, most women and most Native people from owning or inheriting in the first place, so the open field was open only to some. Partial: one way the field stayed unequal named is approaching. (HSS-8.4)"]},{std:["HSS-8.5"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the embargo of 1807 as an attempt at economic pressure in place of war",html:()=>`<p class="big">Britain and France were at war again, and both seized American ships trading with the other. The British navy also stopped American vessels and took sailors off them by force, claiming they were British deserters. Jefferson's answer was the Embargo Act of 1807, which forbade American ships from leaving for any foreign port at all.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>American exports</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1807</td><td>about $108 million</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1808</td><td>about $22 million</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1809</td><td>about $52 million, after the act was repealed</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("anchor",74)}${ico("thermometer",74)}</div>`,qs:["State the theory behind the embargo, and then say what the numbers show about how it worked.","Which Americans were hurt most by it, and why would they call it worse than the problem it was meant to fix?","What does this episode suggest about the tools available to a country that does not want war and cannot win one?"],key:["The theory was that Britain and France needed American food and raw materials badly enough that cutting off trade would force them to respect American shipping, without a shot being fired. The numbers show American exports collapsing by about eighty percent in a single year, which means the pressure fell mainly on Americans. Britain found other suppliers and did not change its policy. Partial: naming the theory with no numbers from the table is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","New England merchants, shipowners, sailors and the shipbuilding trades, along with Southern planters who could not ship cotton and tobacco. They argued that British seizures cost them some ships while the embargo cost them all of them, so their own government had done more damage than the foreign navy. Smuggling became widespread and the act was repealed in 1809. Partial: naming the groups with nothing said about their argument is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","That economic pressure is a real tool and a blunt one. It works only when the other side depends on you more than you depend on them, it hurts your own producers immediately while the effect abroad is slow, and it is hard to enforce. A strong answer notices that the embargo failed on the first of those conditions. Partial: naming the failure with no condition for when such pressure works is approaching. (HSS-8.5)"]},{std:["HSS-8.6"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Northeastern reform movements, the revivals that drove them and the conditions they set out to change",html:()=>`<p class="big">From the 1790s through the 1840s a wave of religious revivals ran through the North, so large that it is called the Second Great Awakening. Preachers such as Charles Grandison Finney held that salvation was a choice any person could make and that a converted person was obliged to go out and improve the world. Upstate New York saw so many revivals it was nicknamed the burned over district. The societies that came out of those meetings went after one problem after another: alcohol, prisons, the treatment of people with mental illness, schooling, shorter working hours, slavery, and the standing of women. In 1841 Dorothea Dix, a Unitarian schoolteacher from Massachusetts, visited a jail to teach a class and found people with mental illness in unheated cells alongside convicted criminals. She inspected every jail and almshouse in the state, handed the legislature a county by county report with names, dates and conditions, and got a hospital funded. She repeated the method in state after state, and more than thirty built or expanded hospitals as a result.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lantern",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Dix could have published a pamphlet of outrage. Why inspect every single institution and write a county by county report instead?","Explain the connection between a religious revival and a campaign about jails or alcohol. Why would one lead to the other?","Not all reform ideas of these years look good now. Give an example of a limit or a blind spot in one of them."],key:["Because legislators can dismiss a general accusation and cannot easily dismiss their own county with specifics attached. A documented survey turned a moral claim into evidence, made each member personally accountable for a place his constituents knew, and gave supporters something to cite. It is a model of how organized evidence moves a legislature. Partial: naming the survey with nothing said about what evidence does to a legislature is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","The revival preaching held that a person chooses salvation and then owes the world the fruits of it, so individual conversion was supposed to produce public work. It also supplied the machinery: congregations already met weekly, had officers, took collections and discussed right conduct, so a reform society could be built out of one in an evening. A strong answer names both the motive and the organization. Add that the Northeast was also urbanizing fast, which put poverty and crowding where they could be seen and counted, and had the most newspapers to argue in. Partial: one of the motive and the organization named is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Any supported example: temperance campaigns often aimed at Irish and German Catholic immigrants and shaded into blaming the poor for poverty; some asylums and reform prisons became custodial institutions where people were confined for decades; reformers frequently assumed the people they helped should not decide their own treatment; and many Northeastern reformers were far more comfortable improving institutions at home than confronting slavery. Partial: an example named with no support is approaching. (HSS-8.6)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Three Economies",short:"Sections",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.7"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the structure of the Southern economy and what it depended on",html:()=>`<p class="big">The two sections in 1860.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Free states</th><th>Slave states</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Share of the nation's factories</td><td>about 85 percent</td><td>about 15 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Share of people living in towns over 2,500</td><td>about 26 percent</td><td>about 10 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Miles of railroad</td><td>about 22,000</td><td>about 9,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Share of the nation's export earnings</td><td>a minority</td><td>cotton alone was over half of all United States exports</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Southern cotton earned more foreign money than anything else the country sold, and the South had few factories. Explain how both can be true at once.","Southern railroads mostly ran from inland cotton country to a port rather than linking cities to each other. What does that pattern tell you?","Where did the South's capital go, and why did that choice limit it?"],key:["Because the South sold a raw material and bought nearly everything else. Cotton went to British and Northern mills and the earnings came back as purchases of cloth, tools, machinery and even food, so the region could be enormously profitable and still have little industry of its own. Earning well and developing are not the same thing. Partial: naming the cotton earnings with nothing said about what the region had to buy is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","That the network was built to export one crop rather than to knit a region together. Towns off the cotton routes stayed isolated, there was no dense interior market, and during the war those lines could not move troops and supplies laterally the way the Northern network could. Infrastructure follows whatever the economy is organized around. Partial: describing the lines with nothing said about what the economy was organized around is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","Into land and into enslaved people, who were the single largest category of wealth in the region. That left less available for factories, schools, canals and city building, and it tied the region's fortune to one crop and one labor system. It also meant any challenge to slavery was a challenge to most of the South's accumulated wealth, which is central to understanding the politics. Partial: naming where the money went with nothing said about what that meant for the politics is approaching. (HSS-8.7)"]},{std:["HSS-8.8"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the California gold rush and its effects on the people already in California",html:()=>`<p class="big">Gold was found at a sawmill on the American River in January 1848. Word reached the East that autumn.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>California's population</th><th></th></tr>
     <tr><td>1848, not counting Native peoples</td><td>about 14,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1852</td><td>about 220,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Native population of California, about 1848</td><td>roughly 150,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Native population of California, 1870</td><td>roughly 30,000</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Arrivals came from the eastern states, from Mexico, Chile and Peru, from China, Ireland and France. California became a state in 1850 without ever being a territory.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("gem",74)}${ico("tent",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why California skipped the usual territorial stage and became a state almost immediately.","The last two rows show the Native population falling by about four fifths in twenty years. Name the main causes.","Most individual miners did not get rich. Who did make money in the gold rush, and why?"],key:["Because the population arrived all at once and needed courts, land titles and a government immediately, and because Congress was deadlocked over slavery in the new territory. Admitting California directly as a free state, as part of the Compromise of 1850, settled the government question and the slavery question for that one place at the same time. Partial: naming the population rush with nothing said about the deadlock over slavery is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","Disease carried by the newcomers, the destruction of the food base as mining tore up rivers and livestock took the grasslands, forced labor and removal under new state laws, and organized killing that state authorities funded rather than prevented. It was a collapse caused by policy and settlement together, not by any single event. Partial: one cause named is approaching, and naming disease alone misses the role of policy. (HSS-8.8)","Merchants, shippers, hotel and restaurant keepers, laundries, and anyone selling tools, food or transport, because prices in the camps were enormous and demand was certain while any one claim was a gamble. Later, companies with capital for heavy equipment took the gold that individuals with pans could not reach. The reliable money was in supplying the miners. Partial: naming the suppliers with nothing said about why that money was reliable is approaching. (HSS-8.8)"]},{std:["HSS-8.9"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the work of Black women abolitionists and the arguments they joined together",html:()=>`<p class="big">Sojourner Truth was born into slavery in New York around 1797. She walked away with her infant daughter in 1826. The next year she went to court and won back her son, who had been sold illegally into Alabama. She could not read or write, so she dictated her life story in 1850 and sold copies to fund her travels, then spent decades speaking at antislavery and women's rights meetings. Other Black women did the same work in different ways. Maria Stewart lectured in Boston in the 1830s. Frances Ellen Watkins Harper wrote poems and gave hundreds of lectures. Harriet Jacobs published a detailed account of what slavery did to women in 1861.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hands",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Truth went to court in 1827 and won. Why is that worth noticing, and what did it require of her?","These women argued for abolition and for women's rights at the same time. Why would they treat the two as one argument?","Several of them funded their work by selling their own books at their own lectures. What does that tell you about how the movement operated?"],key:["Because a formerly enslaved woman who could not read used a state court to force the return of her child from another state and won, which shows that New York's gradual abolition law had real force if someone was willing to use it. It required her to learn what the law said, find people who would help, travel, and persist against an owner with more money and standing. Partial: retelling the case with nothing said about what it shows about the law is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","Because both rested on the same claim: that a person's rights do not depend on who they are. Arguments used to deny Black Americans rights were the same arguments used to deny women rights, so defeating one weakened the other. They also had direct experience of being excluded on both grounds at once, including by allies in each movement. Partial: noting they worked on both with nothing said about the shared claim is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","That it ran on small money raised in person, not on institutions. A speaker paid her own travel out of book sales and collections at the door, which means success depended on persuading ordinary audiences one hall at a time. It also gave the speaker independence, since no committee could withdraw her funding over what she said. Partial: naming the collections with nothing said about the independence it gave is approaching. (HSS-8.9)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Through the War",short:"War and After",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.10"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the home front during the Civil War and the strains it put on both societies",html:()=>`<p class="big">Both governments ran out of volunteers. The Confederacy began drafting men in 1862 and the Union in 1863, and both laws let a man avoid service in ways that favored the wealthy, which produced bitter resentment and riots. Prices in the Confederacy rose so far that by 1864 a barrel of flour in Richmond cost more than a soldier earned in a year, and women organized bread protests in several Southern cities. In the North, women took over farm and factory work and ran the Sanitary Commission, which raised money and supplies on an enormous scale. Clara Barton carried supplies to field hospitals and later founded the American Red Cross.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bread",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("tent",74)}</div>`,qs:["Both draft laws let wealthier men escape service. Why would governments write them that way, and what did it cost them?","Prices rose far more in the Confederacy than in the Union. Explain why.","Name two ways the war changed what work women did, and say whether you would expect those changes to last."],key:["Because both governments needed the cooperation of the men who ran the economy and the political system, and both allowed substitutes or exemptions for particular occupations and, in the Confederacy, for owners of many enslaved people. The cost was legitimacy: ordinary families concluded the war was being fought by the poor for the benefit of the rich, which produced draft riots and desertion. Partial: naming the exemptions with nothing said about the cost in legitimacy is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","The Confederacy had little industry, lost its ports to the blockade, and had almost no way to raise money except printing it, so more and more currency chased fewer and fewer goods. The Union had factories, an intact tax system, bond sales and far more food, so it could pay for the war without destroying its own money. Partial: one of the two sides explained is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Any two: running farms and businesses alone, taking factory and government clerical jobs, nursing in military hospitals, and organizing large relief operations. On whether it lasted, a strong answer argues both ways: many jobs reverted when men returned, but nursing became a recognized paid profession, clerical work stayed open, and the organizing skills went straight into later reform and suffrage campaigns. Partial: two changes named with no answer about whether they lasted is approaching. (HSS-8.10)"]},{std:["HSS-8.11"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Black Codes and why Congress took control of Reconstruction",html:()=>`<p class="big">In 1865 and 1866, Southern legislatures restored under President Johnson's plan passed laws known as the Black Codes.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Typical provision</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
     <tr><td>a freedperson without a labor contract could be arrested for vagrancy</td><td>refusing an offered wage became a crime</td></tr>
     <tr><td>those convicted could be hired out to pay the fine</td><td>unpaid labor returned under another name</td></tr>
     <tr><td>children could be apprenticed to a former owner by a judge</td><td>families lost control of their own children</td></tr>
     <tr><td>freedpeople were barred from many trades and from owning firearms</td><td>work and self defense were restricted by race</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("key",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["These laws never use the word slavery. Explain how they reached a similar result anyway.","Congress responded with the Civil Rights Act of 1866 and then the Fourteenth Amendment. Why pass an amendment when it had already passed a law?","Explain why a vagrancy law, which sounds neutral, was not neutral in practice."],key:["By making the alternatives to working for a former owner illegal. If having no contract is a crime, and the punishment is being hired out, then a freedperson has no real power to refuse terms or to leave, which is most of what slavery consisted of in daily practice. The ownership was gone and much of the coercion was rebuilt. Partial: describing the codes with nothing said about the alternatives being closed off is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Because a law can be repealed by a later Congress, struck down by a court as beyond federal power, or left unenforced by a different president, and Johnson had already vetoed the act. An amendment is far harder to undo and binds the states directly, so it put citizenship, due process and equal protection beyond ordinary politics. Partial: one way a law can be undone named is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Because it was written and enforced against one group. The codes defined vagrancy so that any freedperson not under contract to a landowner qualified, while white men without employment were not arrested, and the local officials deciding who to charge were the same people who wanted the labor. A law applied selectively is a racial law whatever its words say. Partial: quoting the neutral wording without testing how it was applied is approaching. (HSS-8.11)"]},{std:["HSS-8.12"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the growth of organized labor and the political response to it",html:()=>`<p class="big">Workers organized as firms grew.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Organization</th><th>Who it took in</th><th>What it wanted</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Knights of Labor, 1870s and 1880s</td><td>skilled and unskilled, women, Black workers</td><td>the eight hour day, an end to child labor, and cooperatives to replace wage work</td></tr>
     <tr><td>American Federation of Labor, founded 1886</td><td>skilled workers, organized by trade</td><td>higher wages, shorter hours, and written contracts</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">In 1877 and again in 1894 railroad strikes shut down traffic across whole regions, and in both cases federal troops were sent to reopen the lines.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hands",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["The two organizations wanted different things. Describe the difference in their goals, and which one you would expect to last longer.","Why would a railroad strike bring federal troops when a strike at a single factory usually did not?","Workers asked for an eight hour day in an era of ten and twelve hour days. Why was the length of the day the demand they organized around?"],key:["The Knights wanted to change the system, replacing wage labor with worker owned cooperatives, while the Federation accepted wage labor and bargained hard within it for pay, hours and contracts. The narrower goal proved more durable, because it could be won in pieces and delivered visible gains to dues paying members. Partial: one of the two organizations described is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Because railroads carried the mail, troops and the country's freight, so a rail stoppage halted commerce far beyond the companies involved. Governments treated that as an emergency and used the mails and interstate commerce as the legal basis to intervene, which in practice put federal force on the employers' side. Partial: naming the disruption with nothing said about the legal basis for intervening is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Because hours were the one thing every worker in every trade shared, it was easy to state and easy to check, and it directly concerned health, family life and the chance to learn or organize. Wage demands differed by trade and by skill, while a shorter day was a single demand that could unite an entire movement. Partial: naming the demand with no contrast to wages differing by trade is approaching. (HSS-8.12)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Blood That Does Not Freeze",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a change in a gene through the protein it codes for to the trait that results",html:()=>`<p class="big">Arctic char under the depot ice carry four versions of one gene. It codes for a protein that sticks to tiny ice crystals in the blood and stops them growing.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Gene version</th><th>Protein the gene codes for</th><th>Blood stays liquid down to</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A1, unchanged</td><td>full length protein, normal amount, binds ice well</td><td>minus 2.2 degrees Celsius</td></tr>
     <tr><td>A2, one base changed on the ice binding face</td><td>full length protein, normal amount, binds ice weakly</td><td>minus 1.1 degrees Celsius</td></tr>
     <tr><td>A3, the whole gene copied forty times over</td><td>the same protein, forty times as much of it</td><td>minus 2.6 degrees Celsius</td></tr>
     <tr><td>A4, a single base inserted near the start</td><td>everything after the insertion is read out of step, so the protein is nonsense</td><td>minus 0.8 degrees Celsius</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("fish",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain how one inserted base in A4 ruins the whole protein instead of changing one amino acid.","A3 changed no letter of the protein's sequence at all. Explain how it changes the trait anyway.","A2's protein is full length and present in normal amounts, and the fish still freezes a degree earlier. Say why, and what that shows about a protein's shape."],key:["The gene is read three bases at a time, and each group of three names one amino acid. Inserting a single base pushes every group after it along by one, so every amino acid from that point on is read from the wrong triplet. The chain that comes out is not the protein with one substitution; it is a different sequence entirely, and it usually hits a stop signal early as well. One base near the start therefore wrecks the whole protein, while the same insertion near the very end would spoil only the tail. Partial: saying the insertion shifts the reading frame, with nothing said about every amino acid after it being read from the wrong triplet, is approaching. (LS3)","The trait depends on how much working protein the cell has, not only on what the protein's sequence is. Forty copies of the gene are read forty times over, so the blood carries far more of the same ice binding protein, more crystals are blocked, and the freezing point drops further, to minus 2.6 instead of minus 2.2. Changing the number of copies changes the amount, and the amount changes the trait. Partial: saying there are forty copies of the gene, with nothing said about the trait depending on how much working protein the cell has, is approaching. (LS3)","A protein works by shape, and the one base change alters an amino acid on the face that touches the ice. The chain still folds and still reaches the blood, so there is plenty of it, but the face no longer fits an ice crystal properly, so it binds weakly and blocks fewer crystals. A protein's job depends on the shape of the exact surface that does the work, so a single change in the wrong place can cost most of the function while leaving the protein otherwise intact. Partial: saying the protein binds ice less well, with nothing said about the change falling on the face that touches the crystal, is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:27,title:"The Cliff on the Ice Free Coast",short:"Evolution",parts:[{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading a fossil sequence and a nested pattern of shared features as evidence of common ancestry",html:()=>`<p class="big">A cliff on the ice free coast near the depot, recorded layer by layer. Layers pile up newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Rock</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1, 0 to 5 m</td><td>gravel</td><td>seal bones and fish like those alive today</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2, 5 to 16 m</td><td>sandstone</td><td>a swimming mammal with four limbs, the front pair broad and flattened</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3, 16 to 30 m</td><td>mudstone</td><td>a four legged land mammal with a long tail and a bear like skull</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4, 30 to 48 m</td><td>limestone</td><td>lungfish, and the earliest four limbed animals with ribs and a neck</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Features counted across six living animals.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>lamprey</th><th>shark</th><th>salmon</th><th>frog</th><th>lizard</th><th>mouse</th></tr>
     <tr><td>backbone</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>jaws</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bony skeleton</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>four limbs</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>egg with a shell and inner membranes</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>hair and milk</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("seal",74)}${ico("fish",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["Read the layers from oldest to youngest and describe how the limbs change.","Describe the pattern made by the yes answers in the feature chart, and say what kind of relationship that pattern fits.","A seal embryo grows a tail and separate finger bones inside each front flipper before it is born. Say how that completes the argument from the two charts."],key:["Oldest first: layer 4 with lungfish and the earliest animals to have four limbs at all, then layer 3 with a four legged land mammal, then layer 2 with a four limbed swimmer whose front limbs are broad and flattened, then layer 1 with seals and fish of today. Limbs appear, then serve walking on land, then are reshaped into paddles for swimming. The same four limbs are reworked for a new job rather than replaced, and the sequence runs in one direction through time. Partial: the four layers in the right order with nothing said about the same four limbs being reworked rather than replaced is approaching. (LS4)","The yes answers nest: every animal with jaws also has a backbone, every one with a bony skeleton also has jaws, every one with four limbs also has a bony skeleton, and so on up to hair and milk, which only the mouse has. Each feature is found inside the group defined by the feature above it, with no animal picking a feature while skipping an earlier one. That is the pattern of a branching family tree, where a new feature appears once in an ancestor and is then inherited by everything descended from it. Partial: describing the nesting with nothing said about it being the pattern a branching family tree produces is approaching. (LS4)","The embryo builds the ancestral parts before the adult form hides them: separate finger bones inside what becomes a flipper, and a tail the adult does not show. That is what a reworked inherited limb looks like from the inside. The cliff gives the sequence of forms in time, the feature chart gives the nested pattern across living animals, and the embryo shows the inherited instructions still being followed underneath. Fossil, anatomical and developmental evidence all point to common ancestry. Partial: naming the finger bones in the flipper with nothing said about the three kinds of evidence pointing the same way is approaching. (LS4)"]},{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both sets of numbers. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"explaining natural selection from a trait distribution and distinguishing it from artificial selection",html:()=>`<p class="big">Snow buntings nest on the headland. The small soft seeded plants there were crowded out by a tough seeded grass, so most of the seed now available is large and hard. One hundred and sixty birds were measured each generation.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Bill depth</th><th>Generation 1</th><th>Generation 5</th><th>Generation 11</th></tr>
     <tr><td>under 7.0 mm</td><td>62</td><td>34</td><td>11</td></tr>
     <tr><td>7.0 to 8.4 mm</td><td>71</td><td>83</td><td>72</td></tr>
     <tr><td>over 8.4 mm</td><td>27</td><td>43</td><td>77</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The haul dogs are a different story. For ninety years the teams bred only from the dogs that held up best over long distances. The distance a team covers in a day rose from 38 km to 72 km.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bluebird",74)}${ico("dog",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why a change to large hard seeds moves the distribution the way it does. Name what varies, what the seeds do, and what is inherited.","Work out the percentage with bills over 8.4 mm in generation 1 and in generation 11, and explain why the middle row rose and then fell.","Compare the buntings with the haul dogs. Name what does the selecting in each case, and say why inheritance matters in both."],key:["Bill depth already varies among the buntings and is inherited. A deep bill cracks a large hard seed and a shallow one cannot, so with only hard seed available the deep billed birds feed better, raise more young and leave more offspring. Those offspring inherit the gene versions for deeper bills, so the distribution shifts deeper every generation. The seeds do not deepen any bird's bill; they decide which birds get to reproduce. Partial: two of the three steps given is approaching, and the inheritance step is usually the one left out. (LS4)","Generation 1: 27 of 160, which is about 17 percent. Generation 11: 77 of 160, which is about 48 percent. The middle row rose from 71 to 83 as birds from the shrinking shallow end were replaced by the middle of the distribution, then fell back to 72 as the whole distribution kept moving and the deep end became the largest group. A middle band grows while the peak is passing through it and shrinks once the peak has moved beyond it. Partial: working out the 17 and 48 percent with the rise and fall of the middle row not explained is approaching. (LS4)","On the headland the food supply does the selecting, with nobody choosing anything. With the dogs the teams chose the parents directly, which is artificial selection, and ninety years of it moved a day's haul from 38 km to 72 km. Inheritance is what makes either one work: unless the trait is carried in the genes and passed on, the next generation starts where the last one started and nothing accumulates, however hard the selection. Partial: naming the selecting agent in both cases with nothing said about inheritance being what lets the change accumulate is approaching. (LS4)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Sun and Moon at the Depot",short:"Universe",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the record. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining daily, seasonal and lunar cycles and the part gravity plays in orbits",html:()=>`<p class="big">The depot sits at 78 degrees north. The log records how long the sun was above the horizon.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Hours the sun was above the horizon</th></tr>
     <tr><td>March 21</td><td>12.6</td></tr>
     <tr><td>May 15</td><td>24</td></tr>
     <tr><td>June 21</td><td>24</td></tr>
     <tr><td>September 23</td><td>12.3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>November 15</td><td>0</td></tr>
     <tr><td>December 21</td><td>0</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Through all of it the moon runs its full cycle of shapes in about 29.5 days.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sun",74)}${ico("moon",74)}${ico("igloo",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain how the sun can stay above the horizon for 24 hours in May and never rise at all in November, at the same place.","The moon's cycle of shapes keeps to about 29.5 days whether the sun is up all day or never rises. Explain why polar day and polar night do not disturb it.","Name the force that keeps Earth going around the sun and the moon going around Earth, and say which motion sets the day and which sets the cycle of shapes."],key:["Earth's axis is tilted about 23.5 degrees and keeps pointing the same way in space all year. At 78 degrees north the depot lies inside the region that tilt carries right over the top, so for part of the year the whole of the depot's daily circle stays on the sunlit side of Earth and the sun never sets, and half a year later the whole circle stays on the dark side and the sun never rises. One tilt and one orbit give both results. Partial: naming the tilt with nothing said about the depot's whole daily circle staying on the sunlit or the dark side is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","The shapes depend only on where the moon sits along its orbit relative to the sun and Earth, because that is what decides how much of the moon's lit half faces us. The moon takes about 29.5 days to come back to the same position, and that period has nothing to do with whether the sun happens to be above the depot's horizon. Polar day and night change what can be seen from the ground, not where the moon is. Partial: saying the cycle depends on the moon's orbit, with nothing said about polar day and night changing only what can be seen from the ground, is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Gravity in both cases, with its strength set by the two masses and the distance between them. Earth turning once on its own axis sets the day and the rising and setting of everything in the sky. The moon traveling once around Earth, about every 29.5 days relative to the sun, sets the cycle of shapes. Two different motions, which is why one cycle is a day long and the other a month. Partial: naming gravity with only one of the two motions matched to its cycle is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]},{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"comparing the scale of solar system objects and ordering dated layers with a lava flow between them",html:()=>`<p class="big">Five objects, by width.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Object</th><th>Diameter in km</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>1,392,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Jupiter</td><td>139,800</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Earth</td><td>12,760</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the moon</td><td>3,475</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Ceres, the largest asteroid</td><td>940</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The coastal cliff again, dated, with a lava flow in it.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Unit, top to bottom</th><th>Age</th><th>What is in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>loose till left by the ice</td><td>12,000 years</td><td>scratched boulders</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sandstone</td><td>70 million years</td><td>dinosaur bone</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a basalt lava flow lying on the shale and under the sandstone</td><td>120 million years</td><td>no fossils</td></tr>
     <tr><td>shale</td><td>150 million years</td><td>coiled shelled ammonites</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("globe",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Work out roughly how many times wider each object is than the next one down the list.","A model makes the sun a ball 1.4 m across. Work out how wide Earth and Ceres would be on that model.","Put the four units in order from oldest to youngest, and say which two of them pin down the lava flow's age without using its date at all."],key:["The sun is 1,392,000 divided by 139,800, about 10 times Jupiter. Jupiter is 139,800 divided by 12,760, about 11 times Earth. Earth is 12,760 divided by 3,475, about 3.7 times the moon. The moon is 3,475 divided by 940, also about 3.7 times Ceres. Each step down is a factor of roughly 4 to 11, so the five objects span a range of more than a thousand times from end to end. Partial: two or three of the four ratios worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","1.4 m stands for 1,392,000 km, so 1 m stands for about 994,000 km. Earth: 12,760 divided by 1,392,000, times 1.4 m, is about 0.0128 m, or 1.3 cm. Ceres: 940 divided by 1,392,000, times 1.4 m, is about 0.00095 m, which is under 1 mm. A sun the size of a doorway makes Earth a marble and the largest asteroid a grain of sand. Partial: one of the two model widths worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Oldest to youngest: shale, then the lava flow, then the sandstone, then the till. The shale and the sandstone pin the lava down on their own: the flow lies on top of the shale, so it is younger than 150 million years, and the sandstone lies on top of the flow, so the flow is older than 70 million years. That brackets it between 150 and 70 million years, and its measured date of 120 million falls inside the bracket, which is how a date and the order of the layers check each other. Partial: the four units put in order with the 150 to 70 million year bracket not drawn from the layers above and below is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"What the Coast Can Replace",short:"Human Impact",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"separating population growth from per person consumption in total demand on a resource",html:()=>`<p class="big">Fish landed from the cold water shelf the expedition is surveying. The stock can replace about 26,000 tons a year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>People in the region</th><th>kg eaten per person per year</th><th>Total landed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1950</td><td>400,000</td><td>24</td><td>9,600 tons</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1975</td><td>700,000</td><td>31</td><td>21,700 tons</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2000</td><td>1,100,000</td><td>27</td><td>29,700 tons</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2025</td><td>1,500,000</td><td>20</td><td>30,000 tons</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("fish",74)}${ico("speedboat",74)}${ico("wave",74)}</div>`,qs:["Landings more than tripled between 1950 and 2025 while per person use fell after 1975. Separate the two causes using the numbers.","Work out the per person figure that would bring the 2025 total down to the 26,000 tons the stock can replace.","Name two effects on Earth's systems of landing more than the stock replaces for decades, and say which factor in the chart a fishery manager can actually set."],key:["People rose from 400,000 to 1,500,000, a factor of 3.75. Per person use fell from 24 kg to 20 kg, a factor of about 0.83. Multiplied together, 3.75 times 0.83 is about 3.1, and 9,600 times 3.1 is about 30,000 tons, which is the total. Population nearly quadrupled while per person eating dropped about a sixth, so population growth drove the whole increase and the drop per person only slowed it. Partial: naming both factors with only one of the two multipliers worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","26,000 tons is 26,000,000 kg. Divided by 1,500,000 people, that is about 17.3 kg per person per year, which is a further cut of about 13 percent below the 20 kg already reached. Alternatively the same total would support about 1,300,000 people at the 20 kg rate, so the limit can be met by eating less each, by there being fewer people, or by bringing fish in from elsewhere. Partial: getting the 17.3 kg with nothing said about the same limit also being met by fewer people or by imports is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Any two: the adult stock falls, so each year fewer fish spawn and the population can collapse to a level it does not recover from; the biggest, oldest fish go first, which leaves a stock of small young fish that produce far fewer eggs; the animals that feed on those fish, such as seals and seabirds, lose their food supply; and the species that the fish used to control become more numerous, which rearranges the whole food web. A manager can set the quota, which is the total, and can influence per person use through price and labelling, but cannot set the number of people. Partial: two effects named with nothing said about a manager being able to set the quota but not the number of people is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Hauling on the Ice",short:"Forces",parts:[{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"applying the third law to a push between two objects and relating force and mass to motion",html:()=>`<p class="big">A crew member on skis stands beside the loaded sledge on smooth ice and pushes off it once.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Object</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed just after the push</th></tr>
     <tr><td>loaded sledge</td><td>180 kg</td><td>0.25 m/s</td></tr>
     <tr><td>crew member on skis</td><td>75 kg</td><td>0.6 m/s</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The sledge is then towed with a spring scale in the line.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Pull</th><th>Mass</th><th>Acceleration</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>90 N</td><td>180 kg</td><td>0.5 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>180 N</td><td>180 kg</td><td>1.0 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>90 N</td><td>90 kg</td><td>1.0 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>150 N</td><td>60 kg</td><td>2.5 m/s each second</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sled",74)}${ico("skis",74)}${ico("pack",74)}</div>`,qs:["The crew member pushed on the sledge and both moved. Explain with Newton's third law and compare the sizes of the two forces.","Multiply mass by speed for each row of the first chart. Say what you find, then predict the crew member's speed if the sledge had massed 360 kg and still left at 0.25 m/s.","Find the two trials in the second chart that differ only in mass and the two that differ only in pull. Say what each pair establishes, then check trial 4 arithmetically."],key:["Forces come in pairs. The crew member pushed on the sledge, and the sledge pushed back on the crew member with a force equal in size and opposite in direction, at the same instant and for the same length of time. On smooth ice neither is held in place, so both slide apart. Nothing about the sledge being heavier makes its push on the skier any weaker. Partial: saying the sledge pushed back, with nothing said about the two forces being equal in size and opposite in direction, is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Sledge: 180 times 0.25 is 45. Crew member: 75 times 0.6 is also 45. The two quantities of motion are equal in size and opposite in direction, which is what a pair of equal and opposite forces acting for the same time produces. A 360 kg sledge at 0.25 m/s carries 90, so the 75 kg skier would leave at 90 divided by 75, which is 1.2 m/s. Partial: finding both products equal to 45 with the 1.2 m/s prediction not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Trials 1 and 3 hold the pull at 90 N and halve the mass from 180 kg to 90 kg, and the acceleration doubles from 0.5 to 1.0. Trials 1 and 2 hold the mass at 180 kg and double the pull from 90 N to 180 N, and the acceleration doubles from 0.5 to 1.0. Trial 4 fits force equals mass times acceleration: 60 kg times 2.5 m/s each second is 150 N, the pull listed. Partial: naming both pairs of trials with trial 4 not checked against force equals mass times acceleration is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]},{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying what changes a magnetic force, naming the field in a gap, and relating weight to mass",html:()=>`<p class="big">A wire carrying current is laid across the ice and a compass is placed beside it. The needle's swing away from north is measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Distance from the wire</th><th>Swing with 2 A flowing</th><th>Swing with 6 A flowing</th></tr>
     <tr><td>2 cm</td><td>14 degrees</td><td>37 degrees</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4 cm</td><td>7 degrees</td><td>20 degrees</td></tr>
     <tr><td>8 cm</td><td>4 degrees</td><td>11 degrees</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The same 12 kg instrument case, weighed in three places.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Weight on the scale</th></tr>
     <tr><td>at the depot, sea level</td><td>117.6 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on a plateau 4,900 m up</td><td>117.4 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on Mars</td><td>44.5 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("compass",74)}${ico("telescope",74)}${ico("pack",74)}</div>`,qs:["Use the first chart. Name the two things that change the needle's swing, and describe the trend each one follows.","The needle swings as soon as the current is on, and the wire never touches it. Name what reaches the needle, and say what would happen if the current were reversed.","Explain all three weights of the instrument case, and name what lies between the case and Mars that carries the pull."],key:["The current and the distance from the wire. Current: at every distance, tripling the current from 2 A to 6 A roughly doubles to trebles the swing, 14 to 37 degrees at 2 cm and 7 to 20 degrees at 4 cm. Distance: at a fixed current the swing falls as the compass is moved away, 14 to 7 to 4 degrees at 2 A, roughly halving each time the distance doubles. More current and less distance both mean a stronger effect. Partial: naming both factors with the trend described for only one of them is approaching. (MS-PS2)","A magnetic field. The moving charge in the wire sets up a field in the space around the wire, in circles about it, and the needle lines itself up with the field where the needle is. Reverse the current and the field circles the other way, so the needle swings the same amount to the other side of north. The field is a real thing filling the gap, which is how a force is exerted with no contact at all. Partial: naming the magnetic field with no prediction made about reversing the current is approaching. (MS-PS2)","The case holds the same 12 kg of matter in all three places. Weight is the gravitational force on it, set by its mass, the mass of the world, and the distance to that world's center. On the plateau the case is 4,900 m further from Earth's center, so the pull is very slightly less, 117.6 N down to 117.4 N. Mars has about a tenth of Earth's mass and about half the radius, which together give 44.5 N. A gravitational field fills the space between the case and the planet and carries the pull. Partial: explaining two of the three weights, or all three with no field named for the gap, is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Energy on the Haul",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["MS-PS3"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"relating kinetic energy to mass and speed and stored energy to the arrangement of objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">The sledge's kinetic energy was measured on the flat.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed</th><th>Kinetic energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>180 kg</td><td>1 m/s</td><td>90 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>180 kg</td><td>2 m/s</td><td>360 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>90 kg</td><td>2 m/s</td><td>180 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>360 kg</td><td>1 m/s</td><td>180 J</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Stored energy measured for a 25 kg crate held on the ice ramp, and for the spring in a tent guy line.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Stored energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>crate held 3 m up the ramp</td><td>735 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>crate held 6 m up the ramp</td><td>1,470 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>crate held 9 m up the ramp</td><td>2,205 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>guy line spring stretched 4 cm</td><td>0.32 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>guy line spring stretched 8 cm</td><td>1.28 J</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sled",74)}${ico("tent",74)}</div>`,qs:["Compare trials 1 and 2. State how kinetic energy depends on speed and give the factor.","Trials 3 and 4 both come to 180 J by different routes. Show how each gets there, then say what that means for a crew deciding whether to halve the load or halve the pace.","Use the ice ramp rows. State how stored energy depends on height, and explain why 735 J is not a property of the crate on its own.","The spring rows quadruple when the stretch doubles, while the ramp rows only double when the height doubles. Say what the two stores have in common and where they differ."],key:["Doubling the speed from 1 m/s to 2 m/s at the same 180 kg multiplied the energy by four, 90 J to 360 J. Kinetic energy goes up with the square of the speed, so the factor on the energy is the square of the factor on the speed. Partial: reading the 90 J and 360 J off the table with nothing said about the energy going up with the square of the speed is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Trial 3 halves trial 2's mass at the same 2 m/s, so 360 J halves to 180 J. Trial 4 doubles trial 1's mass at the same 1 m/s, so 90 J doubles to 180 J. For a crew it means pace matters more than load: halving the pace cuts the energy to a quarter, while halving the load only cuts it in half, so slowing down saves twice as much of the energy that has to be supplied, and it does not require leaving anything behind. Partial: showing both routes to 180 J with no conclusion drawn about pace against load is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Doubling the height doubled the stored energy, 735 J at 3 m and 1,470 J at 6 m, and trebling it trebled the energy, 2,205 J at 9 m, so stored energy is proportional to the height. The 735 J is not the crate's own: it is the energy stored in the arrangement of the crate and Earth, measured from the bottom of the ramp. Measured from the sea ice below, or from the top, the same crate at the same place would carry a different number, so the reference position has to be named. Partial: describing the proportion to height with nothing said about the 735 J belonging to the crate and Earth together is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Both store energy by moving objects against the force between them, and both give it back when released. They differ in how the amount grows: the pull of Earth on the crate is practically the same at every height, so each extra meter stores the same amount and the total is proportional to the height, while a spring pulls back harder the further it is stretched, so each extra centimeter stores more than the last and the total goes up with the square of the stretch. The rule for one store cannot be assumed for another. Partial: noting that the two rules differ with nothing said about the spring pulling back harder the further it is stretched is approaching. (MS-PS3)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Signals Through the Ice",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"relating amplitude to the energy in a wave and using wavelength and frequency to find wave speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">A hammer is struck on the ice and a pickup 300 m away records what arrives through the ice.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Swing of the hammer head</th><th>Wavelength in the ice</th><th>Cycles per second</th><th>Energy at the pickup</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>2 cm</td><td>7.60 m</td><td>500</td><td>1 unit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>4 cm</td><td>7.60 m</td><td>500</td><td>4 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>6 cm</td><td>7.60 m</td><td>500</td><td>9 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>6 cm</td><td>3.80 m</td><td>1,000</td><td>9 units</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("snowflake",74)}${ico("radio",74)}</div>`,qs:["Trials A, B and C differ only in how far the hammer head swings. Use the three energy readings to state the relationship between amplitude and energy.","Work out the speed of sound in the ice from any row, then compare it with 340 m/s in air and say which signal reaches the pickup first.","Compare C and D. Say what changed, what did not, and why the energy is the same."],key:["Doubling the swing from 2 cm to 4 cm multiplied the energy by four, 1 unit to 4 units, and trebling it to 6 cm multiplied it by nine, 1 unit to 9 units. Energy goes up with the square of the amplitude, so amplitude is the measure of a wave's energy and the only column here that changes it. Partial: saying a bigger swing carries more energy, with nothing said about the energy going up with the square of the amplitude, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Speed is wavelength times cycles per second. Row A: 7.60 m times 500 is 3,800 m per second, and row D gives 3.80 m times 1,000, which is also 3,800 m per second. Sound travels through ice more than eleven times faster than through air, so the signal through the ice arrives first: 300 m takes about 0.08 s through ice and about 0.88 s through air, a gap of eight tenths of a second. Partial: working out the 3,800 m per second with the arrival times through ice and air not compared is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The wavelength halved and the frequency doubled, so the sound is an octave higher, and the speed stayed at 3,800 m per second because the speed is set by the ice rather than by how the hammer was swung. The amplitude did not change, staying at 6 cm, and amplitude is what carries the energy, so the pickup reads the same 9 units. Pitch and energy are separate properties of the same wave. Partial: naming what changed with nothing said about amplitude being what carries the energy is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]},{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Use the chart and the two methods. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading reflection, absorption and transmission from data and comparing a digital with an analog signal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp sends 100 units of light at each material. One meter sits behind it and one beside the lamp.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Material</th><th>Passed through</th><th>Came back</th><th>Neither</th></tr>
     <tr><td>clear ice, 10 cm</td><td>71</td><td>13</td><td>16</td></tr>
     <tr><td>hard packed snow, 10 cm</td><td>8</td><td>79</td><td>13</td></tr>
     <tr><td>orange tent fabric</td><td>23</td><td>46</td><td>31</td></tr>
     <tr><td>black rubber boot sole</td><td>0</td><td>6</td><td>94</td></tr>
     <tr><td>tinted goggle lens</td><td>38</td><td>24</td><td>38</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two ways to report the day's position to base by radio.<br>Method 1: a tone whose pitch rises with the distance covered that day.<br>Method 2: the position sent as letters, each letter built from short and long tones.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("radio",74)}${ico("sunGlasses",74)}</div>`,qs:["Hard packed snow sends back 79 and clear ice only 13, and both are nothing but water. Explain the difference.","The tinted goggle lens passes 38 and absorbs 38. Say why both numbers matter to a crew on bright ice.","A storm fills the radio with crackle. Say which method still delivers the right position and why, then name one thing the crew gives up by using it."],key:["Snow is ice broken into countless grains with air between them, so the light meets an enormous number of ice to air boundaries, and a little is reflected at each one. After many bounces most of the light has been turned back out the way it came, which is why snow is bright white. Clear ice has almost no internal boundaries, so light travels through with little to reflect it. Same substance, different structure, and the structure decides the outcome. Partial: saying snow is made of grains, with nothing said about a little light being reflected at each ice to air boundary, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The 38 that passes is what the crew sees by, and it has to be enough to work and read a map. The 38 absorbed is light the lens took out of the beam, which is what protects the eyes on a surface that sends most sunlight straight back up, and it also warms the lens, which is why a tinted lens can fog or frost. Only 24 is reflected, so the lens is doing its job by absorbing rather than by mirroring. Partial: accounting for one of the two figures is approaching, and the absorbed 38 is usually the one left out. (MS-PS4)","The short and long tones. That is a digital signal: each tone only has to be told apart from silence and from the other length, so crackle that would ruin a pitch reading still leaves the pattern readable and the position arrives exactly as sent. The rising pitch is analog, where the value is the pitch itself, so anything that shifts the tone shifts the reported distance with no sign that it is wrong. What the crew gives up is speed and fineness: the coded message takes longer to send, and it can only report positions the code has symbols for. (MS-PS4) Rating guide for this part, because these three questions sit above the grade-level floor of MS-PS4. 3: the student reads reflection, absorption and transmission off the chart correctly and can say that a coded signal survives noise better than one carried by the value itself. 2: reads the chart correctly but cannot say why the coded signal holds up. 1: does not separate what is reflected, what is absorbed and what passes through. Where the count of right answers and this guide disagree, rate from the guide and write the count in Notes."]}]},{id:33,title:"Runners for Soft Snow",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Read the situation and the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria and constraints and evaluating competing designs against them",html:()=>`<p class="big">The sledges sink into soft new snow, and each time it happens the team loses half a day. There is $700 of spare material at the depot, two days to rebuild before the next haul, and an ice bridge 1.1 m wide that the sledge must still cross. Nothing added may weigh more than 20 kg, because everything is hauled by hand.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Cost</th><th>Build time</th><th>Mass added</th><th>Width</th><th>Sinking in soft snow</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: wider wooden runners</td><td>$240</td><td>10 hours</td><td>9 kg</td><td>1.0 m</td><td>4 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: ski style runners with a steel edge</td><td>$610</td><td>16 hours</td><td>14 kg</td><td>0.9 m</td><td>5 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: an inflatable roller under the bed</td><td>$380</td><td>6 hours</td><td>26 kg</td><td>1.3 m</td><td>2 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D: keep the runners, tow a snowshoe plate behind</td><td>$150</td><td>4 hours</td><td>7 kg</td><td>1.1 m</td><td>8 cm</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sled",74)}${ico("skis",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}</div>`,qs:["Say in one sentence what this design has to accomplish, then label every column of the chart as a criterion or a constraint.","Go down the chart and say which designs break a limit, naming each limit broken.","Choose between what remains and defend your choice from the chart. Say what you would test first."],key:["Achieve: keep the loaded sledge riding on top of soft new snow instead of sinking into it. Criterion, the measure of success: how far the sledge sinks in soft snow. Constraints, the limits on any solution: no more than $700, no more than two days of build time, no more than 20 kg added, and no wider than the 1.1 m ice bridge. Only the sinking column is a criterion; cost, build time, mass and width are all constraints. Partial: a goal stated with cost or added mass filed as criteria rather than constraints is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Design C fails two: 26 kg breaks the 20 kg limit and 1.3 m will not cross a 1.1 m bridge, and it is the best performer at 2 cm, which is exactly why constraints have to be checked before performance. Designs A, B and D all fit every limit. So only C is out. Partial: naming Design C with one of the two limits it breaks is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any of A, B or D, defended from the chart. A is the strongest: 4 cm of sinking, the best of the three that fit, at $240 of $700 and 10 of the available hours, adding only 9 kg and at 1.0 m still inside the bridge. D is the cheapest and fastest at $150 and 4 hours but sinks 8 cm, twice as far as A, so it barely addresses the problem. B costs more, takes longer, adds more mass and performs slightly worse than A, so nothing in the chart recommends it. The thing to test first for A is whether 1.0 m wide runners still steer on hard wind packed snow, since the chart only reports sinking in soft snow. Partial: a design chosen and defended from the chart with nothing named that would be tested first is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]},{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing test data to find which change helps and planning the next round of testing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four runner versions were dragged over the same 100 m of soft snow.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Version</th><th>Runner width</th><th>Runner length</th><th>Load</th><th>Sinking</th><th>Pull needed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>8 cm</td><td>1.6 m</td><td>180 kg</td><td>11 cm</td><td>320 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>16 cm</td><td>1.6 m</td><td>180 kg</td><td>5 cm</td><td>210 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>16 cm</td><td>2.4 m</td><td>180 kg</td><td>4 cm</td><td>190 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>16 cm</td><td>1.6 m</td><td>120 kg</td><td>3 cm</td><td>150 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("pack",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name the one change between versions 1 and 2 and between 2 and 3, and say what each did to the sinking and to the pull.","Version 4 cut the load by a third. Compare the pull that saved with the pull saved by widening the runner, and say what the chart tells the team to do.","Specify a fifth version and say what you expect it to do, then name something the four runs left untested and describe the run that would settle it."],key:["Versions 1 to 2: only the width changed, 8 cm to 16 cm, and the sinking fell from 11 cm to 5 cm while the pull fell from 320 N to 210 N. Versions 2 to 3: only the length changed, 1.6 m to 2.4 m, and the sinking fell from 5 cm to 4 cm while the pull fell from 210 N to 190 N. Each test moved one variable, which is the only reason either result can be credited to a cause. Partial: naming both changes with the effect on the pull not given alongside the sinking is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Cutting the load from 180 kg to 120 kg saved 60 N, from 210 N down to 150 N. Doubling the runner width saved 110 N, from 320 N down to 210 N, and it did so while still carrying the full 180 kg. The chart says to widen the runners rather than lighten the load: widening saves nearly twice as much pull and leaves the cargo on the sledge, while a lighter load means more trips to move the same supplies. Partial: comparing the 60 N with the 110 N and drawing no conclusion, or a conclusion drawn with nothing said about the lighter load meaning more trips, is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any version argued from the chart. The strongest is 16 cm wide and 2.4 m long carrying the full 180 kg, which is version 3, since both of its changes helped and neither required leaving anything behind; predict about 4 cm of sinking and about 190 N of pull. Doubling the width again to 32 cm is worth a try, with a prediction that the gain will be smaller than the first doubling was. The untested variable is the snow itself, because all four runs were on the same soft snow, so the next test should hold the version 3 settings and run them on hard wind packed snow and on wet snow to see whether the wide runner still wins. Partial: a fifth version argued from the chart with no untested variable named and no run described is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Strong and Thorough",std:["RL.9-10.1"],needs:["RL.8.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the exact words for each detail.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Claim: Mira measures the situation carefully before she makes her call. Prove it with strong and thorough evidence. Give at least three details from different parts of the story, and explain what each one shows.</p>${story()}`,key:["Three or more details from across the story, each explained. Examples: “Mira had counted flags all morning. Twenty-six of them,” so she tracks the route as she goes. She lifts the fuel can and reports a range, “One day,” then “Maybe a day and a half,” instead of guessing one number. She knows the exact wording of the station rule taped beside the radio. “Her thumb stayed on the button a moment without pressing,” so she weighs the cost before she speaks. She notices that the wind “had begun to move the loose snow in long ribbons across the surface, which is the way it starts.” (HS RL.9-10.1, strong and thorough textual evidence)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Rate That Changes",std:["F-IF.6"],needs:["8.EE.B.5"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",html:()=>`<p class="big">A weather station measured how deep the drifted snow was at the tent door during one storm. The function h gives centimeters of drift after t hours.</p>${bigTable(["t (hours)","0","2","4","6"],[["h(t) (cm)","0","8","26","60"]])}<ol class="qlist"><li>Find the average rate of change of h from t = 0 to t = 2.</li><li>Find the average rate of change of h from t = 4 to t = 6.</li><li>Is h a linear function? Use your two answers to explain.</li></ol>`,key:["4 centimeters per hour. (8 − 0) ÷ (2 − 0) = 8 ÷ 2 = 4. (HS F-IF.6)","17 centimeters per hour. (60 − 26) ÷ (6 − 4) = 34 ÷ 2 = 17. (HS F-IF.6)","No. A linear function has the same average rate of change over every interval of the same width. Here it is 4, then 9 from t = 2 to t = 4, then 17, so the drift is piling up faster and faster and the graph is not a straight line. (HS F-IF.6)"]},3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Two Wants at Once",std:["RL.9-10.3"],needs:["RL.8.3"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Dr. Oyelaran wants two things that pull against each other. Name both wants and give a detail from the story for each one.</li><li>How does his last line show the way he develops by the end of the scene? What idea about the expedition does that development bring out?</li></ol>${story()}`,key:["He wants to reach the drill site: “Three seasons of grant money had gone into getting a team to that spot,” and he wrote the proposal “himself, twice, after the first one was turned down,” and he argues “Six hours out, six back.” He also wants his team safe: the first thing he asks for is the fuel number, he takes his glove off in deep cold to check the crate himself, and he has no answer to “And then what do we drink?” Needs both wants and a detail for each. (HS RL.9-10.3, a complex character with conflicting motivations)","“The site will still be there in November” shows he has moved from arguing for the trip to accepting the turn-back and backing Mira, which he does without a word when he takes the front traces. It brings out the idea that on the ice a safety rule settles what argument and ambition cannot, and that a season can be given up without the work being lost. (HS RL.9-10.3, how a character develops and advances the theme)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Eliminate Both Ways",std:["A-REI.6"],needs:["8.EE.C.8"],n:2,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Show your steps.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Solve this pair: 3x + 4y = 10 and 5x − 2y = 8.</li><li>Solve this pair: 2x + 3y = 12 and 5x + 7y = 29.</li></ol>`,key:["x = 2 and y = 1, or (2, 1). Steps: multiply the second equation by 2 to get 10x − 4y = 16, add it to the first to get 13x = 26, so x = 2, then 3(2) + 4y = 10 gives y = 1. Check: 5(2) − 2(1) = 8. (HS A-REI.6)","x = 3 and y = 2, or (3, 2). Steps: multiply the first equation by 5 and the second by 2 to get 10x + 15y = 60 and 10x + 14y = 58, subtract to get y = 2, then 2x + 3(2) = 12 gives x = 3. Check: 5(3) + 7(2) = 15 + 14 = 29. Both equations had to be multiplied. (HS A-REI.6)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Compare Two Methods",std:["W.9-10.2"],needs:["W.8.2"],n:1,dir:"Add to the writing on your paper, or say it out loud.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Add two or three sentences to your explanation. Compare two ways a team can stay dry on the move: shedding a layer before hard work, and carrying a spare dry base layer in the sled. Give one advantage and one limit of each, use a transition that makes the comparison clear, and keep a formal, objective tone.</p>`,key:["Analyzes rather than lists: gives an advantage and a limit for each method (shedding a layer costs nothing and works right away, but it means stopping and getting cold for a minute, and it fails once the layer is already damp; a spare dry base layer fixes clothing that is already wet, but it adds weight to the sled and a change of clothes in the wind is slow and risky). Uses a comparison transition such as by contrast, whereas, on the other hand. Keeps a formal, objective tone with no slang and no second person opinion. (HS W.9-10.2, analysis of complex ideas with appropriate transitions and a formal objective tone)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Phrase It Together",std:["L.9-10.1"],needs:["L.8.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each new sentence out loud, then name the phrase or clause you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Combine these two sentences into one using a participial phrase: “The sled tipped on the pressure ridge. It lost two crates.”</li><li>Combine these two sentences into one using a subordinate clause that begins with Because: “The seam split in the cold. The fuel drained into the snow.”</li><li>Combine these two sentences into one using an appositive phrase: “Dr. Oyelaran took the front traces of the sled. He is the team’s glaciologist.”</li></ol>`,key:["“Tipping on the pressure ridge, the sled lost two crates.” Names the participial phrase: Tipping on the pressure ridge. Accept any correct version with a participial phrase. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)","“Because the seam split in the cold, the fuel drained into the snow.” Names it as a subordinate clause. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)","“Dr. Oyelaran, the team’s glaciologist, took the front traces of the sled.” Names the appositive phrase: the team’s glaciologist. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","RL.9-10.1","RL.9-10.3"];const WRITE_STD=["W.8.1","W.8.2","W.8.3","W.9-10.2"];const MATH_STD=["8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1","F-IF.6","A-REI.6"];const LOG_NAMES={readq:"Questions read aloud",passage:"Text read aloud by an adult",repeat:"Directions repeated or reworded",vocab:"Word or phrase explained by teacher",hint:"Hint or first step given",fewer:"Fewer questions",time:"Extra time",scribe:"Adult wrote the student’s words",brk:"Break"};function stopLogs(st){const out=["readq"];if(READ_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("passage");out.push("repeat","vocab","hint","fewer","time");if(WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("scribe");out.push("brk");return out}const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function wt(st){return st.std.includes("W.8.8")?"notes":st.std.includes("SL.8.4")?"say":st.std.includes("W.8.3")?"narrative":st.std.includes("W.8.1")?"argument":"informative"}const G_STORY=[["depot","a stack of food and fuel left out on the ice to be picked up later"],["flag line","a row of flags planted to mark a safe route across the ice"],["kerosene","a thin fuel burned in a camp stove"],["seam","the joined edge of a container, where it can split"],["traces","the straps a person or a dog pulls a sled with"],["grant","money given to pay for research"],["proposal","a written plan asking for research money"],["drill site","the spot where a team bores down into the ice to take a sample"]];const G_ICE=[["sea ice","frozen sea water, which floats on the ocean"],["bore","to drill a hole"],["current","electricity moving through something"],["conduct or carry current","to let electricity pass through"],["floe","a flat sheet of floating ice"],["pressure ridge","a wall of broken ice pushed up where two floes collide"],["average","one number that stands for a whole group of numbers"]];const G_SRC=[["field camp","a small camp set up away from the main station"],["logistics","the planning of supplies and travel"],["forum","a website where anyone can post messages"],["upwind","on the side the wind is coming from"],["trodden","walked on and packed down"]];const G_RADIO=[["convoy","a group of vehicles traveling together"],["snow tractor","a heavy machine that pulls loads across snow"],["segment","one marked stretch of a route"],["towed instrument","a measuring device pulled behind a sled"],["drill","here, a measurement made by boring a hole and dropping a tape"]];const G_WRITE={informative:[["layers","several thin pieces of clothing worn one over another"],["windward","the side the wind blows from"],["porch","a small covered space at the door of a tent"],["frostbite","damage to skin and flesh caused by freezing"]],narrative:[["sledge team","a group of people hauling a loaded sled"],["base camp","the main camp a team works out from"],["radio operator","the person who keeps the radio and takes calls"]]};const G_NAV=[["waypoint","a position stored in a receiver, used as a point to steer toward"],["GPS receiver","a handheld device that works out where it is from satellite signals"],["bearing","a direction given as an angle, such as due north"],["melt channel","a groove in the ice where meltwater has run and thinned it"],["bamboo pole","the light cane a route flag is tied to"],["counterclaim","what the other side of an argument says"]];function glossHtml(st){if(st.id===6)return dl(G_WRITE[wt(st)]||G_WRITE.informative);return dl({4:G_SRC,7:G_ICE,9:G_RADIO,11:G_NAV,12:G_NAV,13:G_NAV}[st.id]||G_STORY)}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that prove your answer"],["strongest evidence","the words that prove the answer best, not just words on the same topic"],["explicit","stated right in the text"],["inference","something the text does not say outright, but that you can figure out from clues"],["dialogue","the words characters say out loud"],["incident","one event in a story"],["propel the action","push the story forward so that something new has to happen"],["reveal","to show something that was hidden"],["motivation","what a character wants, and why"],["theme","the message or big idea of a story"],["thorough evidence","enough details, from more than one place in the text"]];const FRAMES={cite:["The answer is ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____” because ____.","I can infer that ____ because the text says “____.”"],"RL.8.3":["When ____ says “____,” it makes ____ decide to ____.","Because ____ happened, ____ now has to ____.","This line shows that ____ is ____ because ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const k=st.std.includes("RL.8.3")||st.std.includes("RL.9-10.3")?"RL.8.3":"cite";return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${FRAMES[k].map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Verbals: verb forms doing another job</h4>`+dl([["Gerund","ends in -ing and acts as a noun. <em>Hauling is slow work.</em>"],["Participle","often ends in -ing or -ed and describes a noun. <em>The drifting snow hid the flags.</em>"],["Infinitive","to + verb. <em>She hoped to reach camp.</em>"]])+`<h4>Voice</h4>`+dl([["Active","the subject does the action. <em>The crew loaded the sled.</em>"],["Passive","the subject receives the action. <em>The sled was loaded by the crew.</em>"]])+`<h4>Mood</h4>`+dl([["Indicative","states a fact. <em>The wind is rising.</em>"],["Imperative","gives a command. <em>Check the stove.</em>"],["Interrogative","asks a question. <em>Is the wind rising?</em>"],["Conditional","tells what would or could happen. <em>I would go if the ice held.</em>"],["Subjunctive","a wish or something not true. Uses were. <em>If the ice were thicker, we could cross.</em>"]])+`<h4>Phrases and clauses</h4>`+dl([["Participial phrase","a participle plus its words, describing a noun. <em>Tipping on the ridge, the sled stopped.</em>"],["Subordinate clause","has a subject and a verb but cannot stand alone. <em>Because the seam split, the fuel drained.</em>"],["Appositive phrase","renames the noun right beside it. <em>Mira, the field assistant, keyed the radio.</em>"]])+`<p class="small">Keep the same voice and the same mood all the way through a sentence.</p>`}const PLAN={argument:["My claim","What the other side says, and my answer","Reason 1 and the evidence","Reason 2 and the evidence","Conclusion"],informative:["The topic: what I will explain","Group 1 and its facts","Group 2 and its facts","Group 3 and its facts","Conclusion: why it matters"],narrative:["Who, where, and when","The problem or the call","What happened first, next, last","A line of dialogue I will use","How it ended and what it meant"],notes:["Which source I trust, and why","My search terms","My quote or paraphrase","My citation"],say:["My recommendation","My strongest evidence from the table","The drawback","What I need from base camp"]};function planHtml(st){return`<p class="small">Copy these headings onto your paper. Jot two or three words next to each one before you start.</p><ol class="card-list">${(PLAN[wt(st)]||PLAN.informative).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`}const SENT={informative:{s:["A polar field team stays warm by ____.","First, ____.","After that, ____.","This matters because ____.","However, ____.","In the end, ____."],w:["first","after that","because","as a result","however","in the end"]},narrative:{s:["It was ____ when the radio ____.","At first, ____.","“____,” the voice said.","A few minutes later, ____.","By the time ____, ____.","Looking back, I understood that ____."],w:["at first","a few minutes later","suddenly","after that","by the time","finally","looking back"]},notes:{s:["I trust Source ____ more because ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Dr. Hana Lindqvist writes, “____.”","According to Dr. Hana Lindqvist, ____.","Author. “Title.” Journal, date."],w:["according to","writes","states","explains"]},say:{s:["The convoy should take Route ____.","The strongest evidence is ____.","The drawback is ____.","For that reason, ____.","From base camp I need ____."],w:["because","the data show","at or above","by contrast","for that reason"]},argument:{s:["The station should ____ next season.","Some would argue that ____, but ____.","One reason is ____. The memo says ____.","A second reason is ____.","For these reasons, ____."],w:["because","however","although","for example","as a result","for these reasons"]}};function sentHtml(st){const d=SENT[wt(st)]||SENT.informative;return`<ul class="starters">${d.s.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul><h4>Linking words</h4><div class="wordbank">${d.w.map(x=>`<span>${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}const CHECK={argument:["I stated my claim at the start.","I said what the other side claims and answered it.","Each reason has evidence from the memos.","I linked claim, reasons, and evidence.","I kept a formal style.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],informative:["I introduced the topic.","My information is grouped in an order that makes sense.","I explained the facts in my own sentences.","I defined a term the reader would not know.","I used transitions.","I used topic words and a formal style.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],narrative:["I set the scene.","Events are in an order that makes sense.","I used at least two lines of dialogue.","I used details the reader can see, hear, or feel.","I used time words.","My ending reflects on what happened.","I reread it for mistakes."]};function checkHtml(st){return`<ul class="card-list" style="list-style:none;padding-left:0">${(CHECK[wt(st)]||CHECK.informative).map(x=>`<li><label style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-start"><input type="checkbox" style="width:20px;height:20px;margin-top:4px"> ${x}</label></li>`).join("")}</ul>`}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){let e=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-");if(!e||!/^[0-9+\-*/().\s]*$/.test(e))return"";try{const o=(e.match(/\(/g)||[]).length-(e.match(/\)/g)||[]).length;if(o>0)e+=")".repeat(o);const v=Function('"use strict";return ('+e+")")();return Number.isFinite(v)?String(+v.toPrecision(10)):"Error"}catch(x){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","C","+","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Add, subtract, multiply, and divide. To see a rate, divide the distance by the time.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.slice(0,-1);calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const rules=rows=>`<table class="meas">${rows.map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">${r[1]}</td></tr>`).join("")}</table>`;const FORM={"8.EE.B.5":["Proportional relationships and slope",rules([["Slope from two points","m = (change in y) ÷ (change in x)"],["Proportional relationship","y = mx, and the graph is a straight line through (0, 0)"],["Unit rate","the amount of y for 1 of x, which is the slope m"]])+ul(["To compare two rates, write both as “per 1 unit” and compare the numbers.","A graph, a table, and an equation can all show the same rate."])],"8.EE.C.8":["Pairs of linear equations",ul(["<strong>Graphing:</strong> the solution is the point where the two lines cross. Read off (x, y).","<strong>Substitution:</strong> when both are solved for y, set the two right sides equal, solve for x, then find y.","<strong>Elimination:</strong> add or subtract the equations so one variable cancels. Multiply an equation first if you need to.","<strong>One solution:</strong> different slopes, so the lines cross once.","<strong>No solution:</strong> same slope, different y-intercepts, so the lines are parallel.","<strong>Infinitely many:</strong> one equation is a multiple of the other, so it is the same line.","Always check your answer in <em>both</em> equations."])]};FORM["8.NS.A.1"]=["Rational and irrational numbers",ul(["A number is <strong>rational</strong> when it can be written as one whole number over another. Its decimal either stops or repeats.","A number is <strong>irrational</strong> when its decimal never stops and never repeats. The square root of any number that is not a perfect square is irrational, and so is pi.","To turn a repeating decimal into a fraction: call it x, multiply by 10 for one repeating digit, subtract, then solve.","To place a square root, find the two perfect squares it sits between."])];FORM["8.EE.A.1"]=["Properties of integer exponents",rules([["Multiplying powers","a to the m times a to the n = a to the (m + n)"],["Dividing powers","a to the m divided by a to the n = a to the (m − n)"],["Power of a power","a to the m, all to the n = a to the (m times n)"],["Negative exponent","a to the −n = 1 over a to the n"],["Zero exponent","a to the 0 = 1, for any a other than 0"]])];FORM["8.EE.A.2"]=["Square roots and cube roots",ul(["x squared = p has two solutions when p is positive: the positive square root and its negative.","x cubed = p has one solution, the cube root of p.","Perfect squares to know: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, 121, 144, 169.","Perfect cubes to know: 1, 8, 27, 64, 125.","The square root of a number that is not a perfect square is irrational."])];FORM["8.F.A.1"]=["Functions",ul(["A function is a rule that gives each input exactly one output.","If one input has two different outputs, the rule is not a function.","The graph of a function is the set of ordered pairs (input, output).","On a graph, two points directly above each other mean one input with two outputs, so it is not a function."])];FORM["8.F.A.3"]=["Linear and non-linear functions",rules([["Linear function","y = mx + b, whose graph is a straight line"],["m","the rate of change, how much y changes for each 1 that x changes"],["b","the value of y when x is 0"]])+ul(["A function is not linear when equal steps in x do not give equal steps in y. A = s squared is an example.","y = mx with no b added is proportional and passes through (0, 0)."])];FORM["8.G.A.3"]=["Transformations with coordinates",rules([["Translate right a and up b","(x, y) becomes (x + a, y + b)"],["Reflect across the x-axis","(x, y) becomes (x, −y)"],["Reflect across the y-axis","(x, y) becomes (−x, y)"],["Rotate 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin","(x, y) becomes (−y, x)"],["Rotate 180 degrees about the origin","(x, y) becomes (−x, −y)"],["Dilate from the origin by k","(x, y) becomes (kx, ky)"]])+ul(["Translations, reflections, and rotations keep every length and every angle.","A dilation multiplies every length by k and keeps every angle the same."])];FORM["8.G.B.7"]=["The Pythagorean theorem",rules([["In any right triangle","a squared + b squared = c squared, where c is the hypotenuse"]])+ul(["The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, and it is always the longest side.","To find the hypotenuse, add the squares of the legs and take the square root.","To find a leg, subtract the square of the known leg from the square of the hypotenuse, then take the square root."])];FORM["8.SP.A.1"]=["Scatter plots",ul(["<strong>Positive association:</strong> as one quantity goes up, so does the other. <strong>Negative:</strong> as one goes up, the other goes down.","<strong>Linear:</strong> the points lie close to a straight line. <strong>Non-linear:</strong> they curve.","<strong>Clustering:</strong> how tightly the points sit around the pattern.","<strong>Outlier:</strong> a point well away from the pattern. Look for a reason behind it.","Rate of change from two points on the band: the rise divided by the run."])];FORM["A-REI.6"]=FORM["8.EE.C.8"];FORM["F-IF.6"]=FORM["8.EE.B.5"];function formHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>{if(seen.includes(FORM[x][0]))return"";seen.push(FORM[x][0]);return`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`}).join("")}const EX={"8.EE.B.5":[["Compare a graph with an equation",["A line on a graph passes through (0, 0) and (4, 10). Its slope is 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5, so it covers 2.5 km each hour.","A second team has the equation d = 3h. Its unit rate is 3 km each hour.","3 is greater than 2.5, so the second team is faster and its line would be steeper."]]],"8.EE.C.8":[["Solve a pair by substitution",["Pair: y = 4x − 1 and y = x + 8.","Both are solved for y, so set the right sides equal: 4x − 1 = x + 8.","Solve: 3x = 9, so x = 3.","Put x = 3 into either equation: y = 3 + 8 = 11.","Solution (3, 11). Check in the other equation: 4(3) − 1 = 11."]],["Solve a pair by elimination",["Pair: 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1.","Add the two equations so y cancels: 3x = 12, so x = 4.","Put x = 4 back in: 4 − y = 1, so y = 3.","Solution (4, 3). Check: 2(4) + 3 = 11."]]]};EX["A-REI.6"]=EX["8.EE.C.8"];EX["F-IF.6"]=EX["8.EE.B.5"];function exHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>EX[x]).map(x=>EX[x].map(e=>{if(seen.includes(e[0]))return"";seen.push(e[0]);return`<h4>${e[0]}</h4><ol class="card-list">${e[1].map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`}).join("")).join("")+`<p class="small">These are different problems from the ones on the stop. Use the same steps.</p>`}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;const GS=440,GM=10,GU=(GS-68)/(2*GM);function pgridHtml(){const lines=[];if(gridJoin)for(let i=1;i<gridPts.length;i++)lines.push([gridPts[i-1],gridPts[i]]);return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div><div class="figure" data-pg style="cursor:crosshair">${F.coordGrid({max:GM,quad4:true,size:GS,points:gridPts,lines})}</div><p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const svg=b.querySelector("[data-pg] svg");if(!svg)return;svg.onclick=e=>{const q=svg.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*GS/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*GS/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-34)/GU)-GM,y=Math.round((GS-34-vy)/GU)-GM;if(Math.abs(x)>GM||Math.abs(y)>GM)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={gloss:{name:"Word help",std:["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","W.8.8","W.8.1","W.8.2","W.8.3","SL.8.4","RL.9-10.1","RL.9-10.3","W.9-10.2"],html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD,html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],html:gramHtml},plan:{name:"Planner",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:planHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:sentHtml},check:{name:"Writing checklist",std:WRITE_STD,html:checkHtml},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},ex:{name:"Worked example",std:Object.keys(EX),html:exHtml},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:MATH_STD,html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"passage",msg:"If the text or the table was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"terms",std:READ_STD,msg:"The reading terms card defines cite, inference, motivation, and thorough evidence, so this stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"gram",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],msg:"The grammar card defines verbals, voice, mood, and phrase types with examples, so this stop shows using a reference, not recall."},{t:"plan",msg:"The planner supplies the organization, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",msg:"Sentence frames supply the linking words and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"check",msg:"The checklist names what to include. Note that the student used it to revise."},{t:"calc",std:["8.EE.B.5","F-IF.6"],msg:"The calculator divides for the student, so rate whether the student knew to divide distance by time, not the arithmetic."},{t:"calc",std:["8.EE.C.8","A-REI.6"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate the setup and the method, not the computation."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.B.5","F-IF.6"],msg:"The card gives the slope formula and says a proportional graph goes through the origin, so count those questions as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.C.8","A-REI.6"],msg:"The card lists the methods and the three cases for the number of solutions, so count the “how many solutions” question as supported."},{t:"ex",msg:"A worked example models the method. Count the matching questions as supported."},{t:"grid",std:MATH_STD,msg:"If the pair of equations was solved by plotting on the grid tool, note that the student solved by graphing rather than by algebra."},{t:"scribe",msg:"If an adult wrote, rate the ideas and the organization. Do not rate conventions the adult supplied."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1"],msg:"The formula card states the rules, the transformation effects, and the scatter plot vocabulary for this stop, so it shows applying them with a reference, not recall."},{t:"calc",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic, including squares and roots if the student knows to use them. Rate whether the student chose the right method and explained it."},{t:"grid",std:["8.G.A.3"],msg:"If the image was plotted on the grid tool rather than worked out from the coordinates, note that the student transformed by eye rather than by rule."},{t:"terms",std:["RL.8.4","RI.8.8","RI.8.9"],msg:"The reading terms card defines cite, inference, and thorough evidence, so this stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",std:["RL.8.4","RI.8.8","RI.8.9"],msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"plan",std:["W.8.1"],msg:"The planner supplies the claim, counterclaim, reason order, so count the organization and counterclaim checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.8.1"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the counterclaim opener and the linking words, so count those checks as supported."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,logNames:LOG_NAMES,stopLogs}}});
